(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2b.48293e2626.js"]={"25":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Slow waves in intestinal smooth muscle originate in which cells?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","B":"Myenteric ganglion neurons","C":"Submucosal plexus neurons","D":"Enterochromaffin-like cells","E":"Longitudinal smooth muscle cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Costanzo describes the interstitial cells of Cajal, abundant in the myenteric plexus, as the pacemaker of gastrointestinal smooth muscle; their spontaneous depolarizations spread to smooth muscle through gap junctions. Myenteric and submucosal neurons modulate action potentials and force but do not set slow wave rhythm. Enterochromaffin-like cells are gastric histamine cells; it is mucosal enterochromaffin (EC) cells that release serotonin to start the peristaltic reflex. Smooth muscle cells follow the pacemaker rather than generate it. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Slow waves, origin of slow waves); Guyton Ch. 63 (slow waves). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: interstitial cells of Cajal act like the sinoatrial node of the gut and couple to smooth muscle by gap junctions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: unitary smooth muscle lets action potentials spread rapidly from cell to cell (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the intrinsic slow wave frequency of the duodenum?","choices":{"A":"3 per minute","B":"6 per minute","C":"9 per minute","D":"12 per minute","E":"20 per minute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo gives slow wave frequencies from 3 to 12 per minute along the tract, with the duodenum highest at 12 per minute. Three per minute is the stomach, the lowest rate. Nine per minute is the ileum. Six per minute is not a characteristic value given, and 20 per minute exceeds the stated range. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Frequency of slow waves; Small intestinal motility); Guyton Ch. 64 (segmentation maximum near 12 per minute in duodenum, 8 to 9 in terminal ileum). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow wave frequency sets the maximum contraction frequency, so segmentation in the proximal small intestine reaches at most about 12 per minute (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: slow wave frequency falls from duodenum to terminal ileum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"How does parasympathetic stimulation change the electrical activity of intestinal smooth muscle?","choices":{"A":"Higher slow wave frequency","B":"More spikes per slow wave","C":"Larger slow wave amplitude","D":"Shorter slow wave plateau","E":"Longer repolarization phase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Neural and hormonal inputs do not alter the intrinsic slow wave frequency; parasympathetic stimulation increases the number of action potentials on the slow wave plateau and so strengthens contraction. A higher slow wave frequency is the classic trap because that rhythm is set by the pacemaker. Slow wave amplitude, plateau, and repolarization are set by pacemaker calcium and potassium currents and are not described as vagal targets; sympathetic input instead decreases spikes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Frequency of slow waves; gastric and small intestinal motility). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the more action potentials ride on a slow wave, the larger the phasic contraction (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: acetylcholine depolarizes gut smooth muscle and norepinephrine hyperpolarizes it (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main role of segmentation contractions in the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"Propelling chyme toward the cecum","B":"Clearing residue during fasting","C":"Emptying stomach into duodenum","D":"Moving feces into the rectum","E":"Mixing chyme with secretions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Segmentation splits a bolus orad and caudad and then lets it recombine, mixing chyme with pancreatic enzymes and exposing it to the mucosa with no forward propulsion. Propulsion toward the cecum is peristalsis. Fasting clearance is the migrating complex. Gastric emptying and rectal filling by mass movements are other patterns. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Segmentation contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: segmentation mixes and peristalsis propels, both coordinated by the enteric nervous system (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: segmentation movements may still travel about 1 cm anally, so the two patterns overlap (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"During small intestinal peristalsis, what happens to the segment orad to the bolus?","choices":{"A":"It narrows and lengthens","B":"It widens and shortens","C":"It narrows and shortens","D":"It widens and lengthens","E":"It keeps a fixed diameter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts while longitudinal muscle relaxes, so the segment narrows and lengthens. Widening and shortening is the caudad segment, where circular muscle relaxes and longitudinal muscle contracts. Narrowing with shortening or widening with lengthening would need both layers to contract or relax together, which reciprocal innervation prevents. A fixed diameter does not propel the bolus. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excitatory transmitters such as ACh and substance P act behind the bolus, inhibitory VIP and NO ahead of it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: contraction of circular muscle narrows a segment and longitudinal contraction shortens it (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mucosal mediator activates intrinsic primary afferent neurons to start the peristaltic reflex?","choices":{"A":"Somatostatin","B":"Serotonin","C":"Histamine","D":"Glucagon","E":"Secretin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the bolus is sensed by mucosal enterochromaffin (EC) cells, which release serotonin onto intrinsic primary afferent neurons to start the peristaltic reflex. Somatostatin is an inhibitory paracrine. Histamine is a gastric acid secretagogue. Glucagon and secretin inhibit small intestinal motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT from mucosal cells activates IPANs that initiate peristalsis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastrin, CCK, insulin, motilin, and serotonin enhance small intestinal motility, while secretin and glucagon inhibit it (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"At what interval do migrating myoelectric complexes recur during fasting?","choices":{"A":"At 5 minute intervals","B":"At 30 minute intervals","C":"At 90 minute intervals","D":"At 4 hour intervals","E":"At 12 hour intervals"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Costanzo states that migrating myoelectric complexes recur about every 90 minutes during fasting to clear residual chyme from the stomach and small intestine. The shorter and longer intervals do not match the textbook value. Every 5 minutes is far too frequent; fasting migrating complexes recur about every 90 minutes. A 30-minute interval is too short for the roughly 90-minute fasting cycle. A 4-hour interval is too long; the complexes recur about every 90 minutes. A 12-hour interval would allow only one or two complexes per fast, not the regular 90-minute cycle. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric motility; Small intestinal motility; motilin). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the interdigestive complex sweeps from stomach through small intestine every 90 minutes while fasting (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: fasting complexes clear the stomach of residual chyme from the previous meal (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Every 5 minutes is far too frequent; fasting migrating compl","B":"A 30-minute interval is too short for the roughly 90-minute ","D":"A 4-hour interval is too long; the complexes recur about eve","E":"A 12-hour interval would allow only one or two complexes per"}},{"id":"sdl25_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone initiates the interdigestive migrating complexes?","choices":{"A":"Cholecystokinin","B":"Secretin","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Motilin","E":"Peptide YY"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Motilin, secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, initiates the interdigestive myoelectric complexes. CCK and secretin are fed-state hormones that slow gastric emptying. Ghrelin is a gastric hunger hormone, not the initiator of fasting complexes. Peptide YY from ileum and colon inhibits secretion and appetite. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (candidate hormones, motilin). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: motilin is a 22 amino acid candidate hormone outside the gastrin-CCK and secretin families (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: motilin receptor agonists such as erythromycin are used as prokinetics (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"How does eating a meal affect motilin secretion?","choices":{"A":"It inhibits secretion","B":"It stimulates secretion","C":"It doubles the cycle length","D":"It shifts release to the ileum","E":"It has no measurable effect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton states that motilin is released cyclically during fasting and that its secretion is inhibited after ingestion of food, which is why the fasting complexes stop in the fed state. Stimulation is the opposite effect. Cycle length doubling and an ileal shift are not described; motilin comes from stomach and upper duodenum. Sources: Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin secreted during fasting). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: feeding replaces fasting complexes with segmentation and peristalsis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: motilin is secreted by the stomach and upper duodenum, not the ileum (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"How often do colonic mass movements occur?","choices":{"A":"Ten to twelve times per minute","B":"Three to five times per hour","C":"Twenty to thirty times per day","D":"Once or twice per week","E":"One to three times per day"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo states that mass movements move colonic contents long distances, such as transverse to sigmoid colon, one to three times per day. Ten to twelve per minute resembles duodenal slow wave rhythm. The hourly and frequent daily options are too often, and once or twice per week too rare. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Mass movements); Guyton Ch. 64 (series of mass movements lasting 10 to 30 minutes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a final mass movement propels feces into the rectum and produces the urge to defecate (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: during a mass movement the colon loses haustrations and contracts as a unit (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"The efferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex is mediated by which hormones?","choices":{"A":"Secretin and GIP","B":"Motilin and ghrelin","C":"CCK and gastrin","D":"Somatostatin and PYY","E":"VIP and nitric oxide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Costanzo describes gastric distention as the afferent limb, carried by the parasympathetic nervous system, and CCK and gastrin as the efferent limb that increases colonic motility and mass movements. Secretin and GIP decrease contractions. Motilin drives fasting complexes. Somatostatin and peptide YY are inhibitory. VIP and NO are inhibitory neurocrines. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Gastrocolic reflex). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastrocolic reflex explains the urge to defecate soon after a meal (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastrocolic and duodenocolic reflexes nearly disappear when extrinsic autonomic nerves to the colon are removed (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"Which combined contraction forms colonic haustrations?","choices":{"A":"Circular and teniae coli relaxation","B":"Muscularis mucosae fold contraction","C":"External anal sphincter contraction","D":"Circular and teniae coli contraction","E":"Ileocecal sphincter tonic contraction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton describes about 2.5 cm of circular muscle constricting while the teniae coli, the three longitudinal strips, also contract, so the unstimulated wall bulges into baglike haustrations. Relaxation of both layers would not form sacs. The muscularis mucosae folds the mucosa, and the anal and ileocecal sphincters are not involved. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Mixing movements, haustrations); Costanzo Ch. 8 (segmentation in the cecum and proximal colon). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustral contractions peak in about 30 seconds and fade over the next 60 seconds (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: slow haustral mixing takes 8 to 15 hours to move chyme from the ileocecal valve through the colon (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"What does rectal distention cause in the rectosphincteric reflex?","choices":{"A":"Relaxation of the external anal sphincter","B":"Contraction of the internal anal sphincter","C":"Relaxation of the internal anal sphincter","D":"Relaxation of the ileocecal sphincter","E":"Contraction of the ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As the rectum fills, its smooth muscle contracts and the internal anal sphincter relaxes in the rectosphincteric reflex. The external sphincter stays tonically contracted until it is relaxed voluntarily. Internal sphincter contraction is the opposite response. Ileocecal sphincter contraction follows entry of chyme into the cecum and is unrelated to rectal filling. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: defecation is delayed after internal sphincter relaxation because the striated external sphincter remains contracted (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the internal anal sphincter is a tonic smooth muscle sphincter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"At what degree of rectal filling is the urge to defecate felt?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent of capacity","B":"About 25 percent of capacity","C":"About 50 percent of capacity","D":"About 75 percent of capacity","E":"About 90 percent of capacity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the urge to defecate arises once the rectum fills to 25 percent of its capacity. The other values do not match the textbook. Five percent filling is too little; the urge arises at about 25 percent of rectal capacity. Fifty percent overshoots the threshold, since the urge begins at about 25 percent filling. Seventy-five percent is far beyond the ~25 percent filling that triggers the urge. Ninety percent is near full capacity, well past the ~25 percent urge threshold. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Valsalva maneuver raises intraabdominal pressure to aid defecation (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: a weak functional sphincter at the rectosigmoid junction keeps the rectum empty most of the time (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Five percent filling is too little; the urge arises at about","C":"Fifty percent overshoots the threshold, since the urge begin","D":"Seventy-five percent is far beyond the ~25 percent filling t","E":"Ninety percent is near full capacity, well past the ~25 perc"}},{"id":"sdl25_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerve controls the external anal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","B":"Hypogastric nerves","C":"Vagus nerve","D":"Inferior mesenteric nerves","E":"Pudendal nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton states that the external anal sphincter, striated voluntary muscle, is controlled by the somatic pudendal nerve. Pelvic splanchnic nerves carry sacral parasympathetic fibers that reinforce the defecation reflex. Hypogastric and inferior mesenteric nerves are sympathetic. The vagus does not reach the anal canal. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (external anal sphincter is striated). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the external anal sphincter is striated voluntary muscle, unlike the smooth muscle internal sphincter (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic outflow comes from cord segments S2 to S4 (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has abdominal distention and no flatus 2 days after an open colectomy. Temperature is 37.2°C, pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. Bowel sounds are absent. Serum potassium is 4.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Heightened sympathetic outflow to the gut is suspected. Which change in his intestinal smooth muscle electrical activity is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Faster pacemaker slow wave rhythm","B":"Slower pacemaker slow wave rhythm","C":"Fewer spikes on each slow wave","D":"More spikes on each slow wave","E":"Loss of gap junction coupling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic activity and norepinephrine hyperpolarize gut smooth muscle and decrease the number of action potentials on each slow wave, weakening contractions. Slow wave frequency is intrinsic to the pacemaker and is not changed by neural input, so faster or slower rhythms are traps. More spikes is the parasympathetic effect. Gap junction coupling is not altered by sympathetic input. The normal potassium excludes hypokalemia as a cause of the ileus. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (frequency of slow waves; small intestinal motility, sympathetic activity decreases contraction); Guyton Ch. 63 (norepinephrine hyperpolarizes; peritoneointestinal reflex in Ch. 64). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peritoneal irritation inhibits excitatory enteric nerves and can cause intestinal paralysis (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sympathetic fibers to the small intestine arise from the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — sympathetic and parasympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman develops constipation and dry mouth 3 weeks after starting a drug for urge incontinence that blocks muscarinic receptors. She has no abdominal pain. Abdominal examination shows mild distention with hypoactive bowel sounds. Serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL). Reduced action of which transmitter on intestinal smooth muscle best explains her constipation?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine","B":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Somatostatin","E":"Acetylcholine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Muscarinic blockade removes the excitatory action of acetylcholine released by parasympathetic and enteric neurons, so fewer action potentials ride on each slow wave and contractions weaken. Norepinephrine, VIP, nitric oxide, and somatostatin are inhibitory, so reducing their action would increase motility. Normal calcium excludes hypercalcemia. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic stimulation increases contraction); Guyton Ch. 63 (acetylcholine excites; norepinephrine inhibits); Guyton Ch. 64 (atropine weakens segmentation). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anticholinergic adverse effects include constipation, dry mouth, urinary retention, and mydriasis (First Aid Pharmacology, muscarinic antagonists). COMLEX: postganglionic parasympathetic neurons sit in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic neurotransmitters and effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has 2 years of bloating, vomiting, and abdominal distention without mechanical obstruction on imaging. Full-thickness jejunal biopsy shows normal ganglion cells but a marked loss of c-KIT positive cells around the myenteric plexus. Serum TSH is 2.1 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–5.0 μU/mL). Loss of which function most likely explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal serotonin release","B":"Pacemaker slow wave generation","C":"Inhibitory nitrergic signaling","D":"Sympathetic ganglion output","E":"Somatic sphincter control"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The c-KIT positive cells around the myenteric plexus are interstitial cells of Cajal, which generate slow waves and pass them to smooth muscle through gap junctions; without pacemaker activity, coordinated contractions fail and pseudo-obstruction follows. Ganglion cells are normal, so nitrergic inhibition and enteric circuits are intact. Serotonin release is a mucosal enterochromaffin (EC) cell function. Sympathetic and somatic outputs are extrinsic. Normal TSH excludes hypothyroidism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (origin of slow waves); Guyton Ch. 63 (slow waves). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow waves are not action potentials but oscillations in membrane potential set by pacemaker cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: slow wave depolarization is due to inward calcium current and repolarization to outward potassium current (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system regulation of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man develops profuse watery diarrhea and cramping 12 hours after eating undercooked chicken at a picnic. Temperature is 38.3°C and pulse is 104/min. Barium given earlier for another reason traveled from the jejunum to the colon within minutes. Which motility pattern best explains this rapid transit?","choices":{"A":"Peristaltic rush","B":"Segmentation contractions","C":"Migrating motor complex","D":"Haustral contractions","E":"Gastroileal reflex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intense mucosal irritation, as in severe infectious diarrhea, triggers powerful rapid peristalsis, the peristaltic rush, that sweeps contents into the colon within minutes to relieve the small intestine. Segmentation mixes without much propulsion. The migrating complex is a fasting pattern that travels slowly. Haustral contractions are colonic mixing movements. The gastroileal reflex intensifies ileal peristalsis after a meal but does not cause minute-scale transit. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Peristaltic rush; peristalsis); Costanzo Ch. 8 (peristaltic contractions). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal small intestinal peristalsis moves chyme only about 1 cm/min, taking 3 to 5 hours from pylorus to ileocecal valve (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: peristaltic rush is driven by autonomic and brainstem reflexes plus intrinsic myenteric reflexes (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman in a drug trial receives an agent that inhibits neuronal nitric oxide synthase in the enteric plexuses. Over the next week she reports cramping and slowed bowel transit. Pulse is 72/min and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Abdominal examination shows mild diffuse tenderness. Failure of which step of the peristaltic reflex best explains her slowed transit?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of circular muscle behind the bolus","B":"Contraction of longitudinal muscle ahead of the bolus","C":"Relaxation of circular muscle ahead of the bolus","D":"Release of serotonin from mucosal cells","E":"Slow wave generation in pacemaker cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In front of the bolus, inhibitory pathways that release VIP and nitric oxide relax circular muscle, so the segment widens and receives the bolus. Blocking NO synthesis leaves that segment narrow, so propulsion stalls and cramping follows. Circular contraction behind the bolus and longitudinal contraction ahead of it use excitatory transmitters. Serotonin release and pacemaker slow waves do not depend on neuronal NO. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIP and NO are inhibitory enteric neurocrines that relax gut smooth muscle (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: reciprocal innervation keeps circular and longitudinal layers from contracting together (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — esophageal peristalsis and its neural control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with abdominal cramping takes a drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. Jejunal manometry before the dose shows ring contractions at about 11 per minute that split and recombine chyme. After the dose, contractions at the same rhythm become very weak, and the slow wave frequency is unchanged. Serum magnesium is 2.0 mg/dL (reference range: 1.5–2.5 mg/dL). This change shows that his mixing contractions depend on which input?","choices":{"A":"Circulating motilin from the duodenum","B":"Excitation from the myenteric plexus","C":"Sympathetic outflow from the celiac ganglion","D":"Voluntary outflow from the sacral cord","E":"Stretch afferents of the colonic wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton notes that segmentation becomes very weak when atropine blocks enteric excitation; slow waves set the rhythm but contractions are not effective without background excitation, mainly from the myenteric plexus. Motilin drives fasting complexes rather than fed mixing. Sympathetic outflow inhibits contraction. Voluntary sacral outflow controls the external anal sphincter. Colonic stretch signals affect the ileocecal sphincter, not jejunal mixing. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Mixing contractions, segmentation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (segmentation contractions). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the terminal ileum the maximum segmentation rate is about 8 to 9 per minute (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: segmentation exposes chyme to pancreatic enzymes and the mucosa for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system and extrinsic modulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing type 1 diabetes has early satiety and postprandial vomiting. Gastric emptying scintigraphy shows marked delay. He is started on a low dose of a macrolide antibiotic for its prokinetic effect, and his symptoms improve. Which drug action most likely explains his improvement?","choices":{"A":"Dopamine D2 antagonism","B":"Muscarinic receptor agonism","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 antagonism","D":"Mu opioid receptor agonism","E":"Motilin receptor agonism"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Erythromycin, a macrolide, acts as a motilin receptor agonist and triggers migrating motor complex activity, promoting gastric and small intestinal emptying. Dopamine D2 antagonism is the metoclopramide mechanism. Muscarinic agonism is bethanechol. 5-HT3 antagonists are antiemetics and slow transit. Mu opioid agonists slow motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin initiates interdigestive complexes); Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI hormones, motilin receptor agonists). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist prokinetic that can cause tardive dyskinesia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, metoclopramide). COMLEX: motilin is released cyclically during fasting and suppressed by eating (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis has chronic bloating and loose stools. A lactulose breath test shows an early rise in hydrogen. Fasting antroduodenal manometry shows no phase III activity over 6 hours. Serum albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Loss of which function most likely contributed to her condition?","choices":{"A":"Postprandial mixing of jejunal chyme","B":"Rectal storage of formed feces","C":"Colonic absorption of luminal water","D":"Interdigestive clearance of residue","E":"Gastrocolic reflex after meals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Absent phase III activity means the migrating motor complex is lost; these fasting contractions recur about every 90 minutes and sweep residual chyme and bacteria from the small intestine, so their loss allows bacterial overgrowth, shown by the early hydrogen rise. Postprandial mixing is segmentation. Rectal storage, colonic water absorption, and the gastrocolic reflex are colonic functions. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (migrating myoelectric complexes clear residual chyme); Guyton Ch. 63 (interdigestive complexes every 90 minutes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: small intestinal bacterial overgrowth causes bloating, diarrhea, and a positive hydrogen breath test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption). COMLEX: the interdigestive complex moves through the stomach and small intestine in a fasting person (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — pathogenesis of malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman in a fasting motility study has a jejunal recording showing bands of intense contractions that start in the stomach, travel down the small intestine, and recur periodically throughout the fast. At the 6-hour mark she eats breakfast. Blood glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL) before the meal. Which change in her recording is most likely after the meal?","choices":{"A":"Shortening of the complex interval","B":"Spread of complexes to the colon","C":"Interruption of the complex cycle","D":"Increase in phase III force","E":"Doubling of slow wave frequency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Interdigestive complexes are a fasting pattern; food ingestion inhibits motilin secretion, so the cycle is interrupted and replaced by fed segmentation and peristalsis. Eating does not shorten the interval or strengthen phase III. Complexes do not extend into the colon. Slow wave frequency is intrinsic and does not change with feeding. Sources: Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin secretion inhibited after food; interdigestive complexes); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin; small intestinal motility). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peristalsis rises after a meal through duodenal stretch and the gastroenteric reflex (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: motilin is a candidate hormone secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — hormonal control of gastric and duodenal motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman reports an urge to have a bowel movement within 20 minutes after almost every breakfast. She has no weight loss, blood in the stool, or nocturnal symptoms. Physical examination is normal. Hemoglobin is 13.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL). Which reflex best explains the timing of her urge?","choices":{"A":"Gastroileal reflex","B":"Rectosphincteric reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Gastrocolic reflex","E":"Peritoneointestinal reflex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastric distention by food increases colonic motility and mass movements through the gastrocolic reflex, which pushes feces into the rectum and produces the urge soon after a meal. The gastroileal reflex empties the ileum into the cecum. The rectosphincteric reflex follows rectal filling rather than a meal. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying. The peritoneointestinal reflex inhibits motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Gastrocolic reflex); Guyton Ch. 64 (gastrocolic and duodenocolic reflexes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an exaggerated gastrocolic reflex contributes to postprandial urgency in irritable bowel syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: the afferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex is parasympathetic (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes and duodenal stimuli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has 8 to 10 bloody stools a day during a flare. Temperature is 37.9°C and pulse is 102/min. Hemoglobin is 11.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and C-reactive protein is 48 mg/L (reference range: 0–10 mg/L). Which colonic motility pattern is most likely persistently increased in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Haustral mixing","B":"Mass movements","C":"Migrating complexes","D":"Jejunal segmentation","E":"Reverse peristalsis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton notes that irritation of the colon, as in ulcerative colitis, initiates intense mass movements that persist almost all the time, which drives frequent stools. Haustral mixing is lost during mass movements and in active colitis. Migrating complexes and jejunal segmentation are small intestinal patterns. Retrograde peristalsis belongs to vomiting. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (mass movements, ulcerative colitis); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Mass movements). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis begins in the rectum and extends proximally in continuous fashion (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel disease). COMLEX: a series of mass movements lasts 10 to 30 minutes and may recur half a day later (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — lymphatic drainage and ulcerative colitis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has a barium enema for mild constipation. Images show the cecum and ascending colon divided into regular saclike bulges that shift position over a few minutes, with no mass lesion. Thyroid studies and serum calcium are within normal limits. Which function do these contractions mainly serve?","choices":{"A":"Mixing contents for water absorption","B":"Propelling feces into the rectum","C":"Clearing residue during fasting","D":"Relaxing the internal anal sphincter","E":"Preventing backflow into the ileum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The saclike bulges are haustra from segmentation contractions of the cecum and proximal colon, which mix contents and expose them to the mucosa for water and electrolyte absorption. Propulsion into the rectum is a mass movement. Fasting clearance is the small intestinal migrating complex. Internal sphincter relaxation is the rectosphincteric reflex. The ileocecal sphincter prevents reflux into the ileum. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (large intestinal segmentation contractions, haustra); Guyton Ch. 64 (haustrations). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: teniae coli are three longitudinal muscle bands that create haustra (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: water absorption in the distal colon makes feces semisolid and harder to move (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has abdominal distention and bilious vomiting. He was born at term to a mother with an uncomplicated pregnancy. Digital rectal examination is followed by an explosive release of stool. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Absence of which cells in the distal colon most likely explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","B":"Striated sphincter muscle fibers","C":"Enterochromaffin cells of the mucosa","D":"Sacral preganglionic parasympathetic neurons","E":"Ganglion cells of the myenteric plexus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Delayed meconium, distention, and explosive stool after rectal examination indicate Hirschsprung disease, a lack of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon. Without the intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex, the aganglionic segment stays contracted and the internal sphincter does not relax. Pacemaker cells, striated sphincter fibers, and mucosal endocrine cells are present. Sacral preganglionic neurons lie in the cord, not the colon wall. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Hirschsprung disease). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease results from failed neural crest migration and is associated with Down syndrome and RET mutations (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: rectal suction biopsy is diagnostic, showing absent ganglion cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — myenteric and submucosal plexuses","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman has fecal urgency and leakage 4 months after a prolonged forceps-assisted vaginal delivery. Anal manometry shows a normal resting pressure but a markedly reduced voluntary squeeze pressure. Neurologic examination of the lower limbs is normal. Urinalysis is unremarkable. Dysfunction of which structure most likely explains her findings?","choices":{"A":"External anal sphincter","B":"Internal anal sphincter","C":"Rectal circular muscle","D":"Sigmoid colon teniae","E":"Ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Voluntary squeeze pressure reflects the striated external anal sphincter, controlled by the somatic pudendal nerve, which can be stretched during difficult delivery. Resting pressure comes mainly from the tonically contracted smooth muscle internal sphincter, which is normal here. Rectal circular muscle and sigmoid teniae propel feces. The ileocecal sphincter is not involved in continence. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation, external sphincter and pudendal nerve); Costanzo Ch. 8 (tonic internal anal sphincter; striated external sphincter). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pudendal nerve can be blocked at the ischial spine for vaginal delivery (First Aid Reproductive, pudendal nerve). COMLEX: the external sphincter is held contracted subconsciously unless conscious signals inhibit it (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has worsening constipation and weak, ineffective attempts at defecation 6 weeks after a sacral fracture that damaged the S2 to S4 nerve roots. Rectal examination shows a large amount of soft stool in the vault. Serum potassium is 4.3 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Loss of which pathway most likely explains his weak rectal propulsion?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic fibers in the hypogastric nerves","B":"Vagal fibers supplying the proximal colon","C":"Intrinsic myenteric fibers in the rectum","D":"Parasympathetic fibers in the pelvic nerves","E":"Somatic fibers to the abdominal wall muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton describes the intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex as weak and dependent on reinforcement by a parasympathetic reflex through sacral cord segments; pelvic nerve fibers intensify peristalsis in the descending colon, sigmoid, and rectum and relax the internal sphincter. Damage to S2 to S4 roots removes that reinforcement. Hypogastric fibers are sympathetic and inhibitory. The vagus supplies proximal gut. The intrinsic plexus is intact. Abdominal wall nerves arise higher. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation reflexes, Fig. 64.6); Guyton Ch. 63 (sacral parasympathetics S2 to S4). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: defecation signals entering the cord also trigger a deep breath, glottic closure, and abdominal muscle contraction (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic fibers via the pelvic nerves supply the distal colon, rectum, and anus (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerve parasympathetic distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man develops constipation after starting a drug for angina that slows atrioventricular nodal conduction and relaxes vascular smooth muscle. Which current underlying intestinal slow wave depolarization does this drug most directly reduce?","choices":{"A":"Inward sodium current","B":"Inward calcium current","C":"Outward potassium current","D":"Outward chloride current","E":"Inward magnesium current"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo attributes the depolarizing phase and plateau of the slow wave to cyclic opening of calcium channels with inward Ca2+ current; the drug described, a nondihydropyridine L-type calcium channel blocker such as verapamil, blunts that depolarization and contraction. Outward potassium current repolarizes the slow wave. Sodium, chloride, and magnesium currents are not described as the basis of the slow wave. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Mechanism of slow waves). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: constipation is a common adverse effect of nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as verapamil (First Aid Cardiovascular, calcium channel blockers). COMLEX: even subthreshold slow waves produce weak basal contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old healthy volunteer eats a radiolabeled meal for a transit study. About how long does chyme normally take to travel from the pylorus to the ileocecal valve?","choices":{"A":"10 to 20 minutes","B":"30 to 45 minutes","C":"3 to 5 hours","D":"12 to 18 hours","E":"2 to 4 days"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton states that small intestinal peristaltic waves are weak and die out after 3 to 5 cm, so net movement averages about 1 cm/min and passage from pylorus to ileocecal valve takes 3 to 5 hours. Minutes describe a peristaltic rush. Twelve hours to days fit colonic transit. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Peristalsis in the small intestine). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: peristaltic waves move at 0.5 to 2.0 cm/sec, faster proximally than distally (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: villous contractions driven by the submucosal plexus milk lymph from the central lacteals (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has an overnight fasting manometry study. Cyclic bands of strong contractions recur periodically throughout the fast. Over which part of the gut do these bands travel?","choices":{"A":"Stomach through small intestine","B":"Esophagus through duodenum","C":"Cecum through sigmoid colon","D":"Duodenum through rectum","E":"Sigmoid colon through anus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton describes the interdigestive myoelectric complexes as waves that move through the stomach and small intestine every 90 minutes in a fasting person, clearing residual chyme. They do not begin in the esophagus and do not run through the colon or rectum, where haustral contractions and mass movements dominate. Sources: Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin, interdigestive complexes); Costanzo Ch. 8 (migrating myoelectric complexes in stomach and small intestine). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of fasting complexes predisposes to small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI hormones). COMLEX: motilin is secreted by the stomach and upper duodenum (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with an end ileostomy after colectomy notices that his stoma output rises sharply within minutes of each meal. Which reflex best explains this increase?","choices":{"A":"Gastrocolic reflex","B":"Enterogastric reflex","C":"Rectosphincteric reflex","D":"Renointestinal reflex","E":"Gastroileal reflex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton describes a gastroileal reflex in which eating intensifies ileal peristalsis and forces chyme out of the ileum; with an ileostomy that chyme appears as stoma output. The gastrocolic reflex acts on the colon, which has been removed. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying. The rectosphincteric reflex needs a rectum. The renointestinal reflex inhibits motility. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Peristalsis in the small intestine; ileocecal valve emptying). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ileocecal sphincter normally holds chyme in the ileum, prolonging absorption (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: about 1500 to 2000 mL of chyme empties into the cecum each day (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with chronic constipation is taught to aid defecation by bearing down. Which action most directly increases his intraabdominal pressure during this effort?","choices":{"A":"Relaxing the abdominal wall","B":"Contracting the external anal sphincter","C":"Breathing rapidly through the mouth","D":"Expiring against a closed glottis","E":"Inspiring through an open glottis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo states that the intraabdominal pressure for defecation can be increased by the Valsalva maneuver, expiring against a closed glottis, and Guyton adds deep inspiration and abdominal wall contraction. Relaxing the abdominal wall lowers pressure. Contracting the external sphincter opposes defecation. Rapid breathing and inspiration through an open glottis do not build pressure. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation); Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation reflexes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: straining with a Valsalva maneuver raises intraabdominal and intrathoracic pressure and transiently lowers venous return (First Aid Cardiovascular, maneuvers). COMLEX: people who often inhibit natural defecation reflexes become constipated (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has marked cecal distention from an obstructing sigmoid tumor. Which reflex response is expected where the small intestine joins the large intestine?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of the ileocecal sphincter","B":"Intensified peristalsis in the ileum","C":"Faster emptying of ileal chyme","D":"Increased jejunal segmentation","E":"Relaxation of the internal sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton states that cecal distention intensifies contraction of the ileocecal sphincter and inhibits ileal peristalsis, delaying further emptying into the cecum; these reflexes run through the myenteric plexus and extrinsic nerves. Intensified ileal peristalsis and faster emptying are the opposite responses. Jejunal segmentation and internal anal sphincter relaxation are not part of this reflex. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Feedback control of the ileocecal sphincter). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: large bowel obstruction is most often caused by colorectal cancer in older adults (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal obstruction). COMLEX: an inflamed appendix can cause ileocecal sphincter spasm and partial ileal paralysis (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"grid_sdl25_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":4,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has a complete spinal cord transection at T6 from a motorcycle crash 1 year ago; spinal shock has resolved. He has episodes of reflex bowel emptying at unpredictable times. Serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which of the following sets of anorectal findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Reflex IAS relaxation","Voluntary EAS squeeze","Rectal fullness sensation"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↔","↔"],"B":["↔","↓","↔"],"C":["↔","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↓"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↓ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↓ rectal fullness sensation) is correct. The intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex and the sacral parasympathetic reflex through the pelvic nerves lie below the T6 lesion, so rectal distention still relaxes the internal anal sphincter, and Guyton notes that people with transected cords have automatic emptying of the lower bowel. Conscious control of the external anal sphincter through the pudendal nerve needs descending cortical input, and the sensation of rectal fullness needs ascending tracts, so both are lost. Choice A (↓ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is Hirschsprung disease, in which aganglionic bowel fails to relax the internal sphincter. Choice B (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↓ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is pudendal nerve injury, as after a difficult vaginal delivery. Choice D (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is normal anorectal function, as in functional constipation. Choice E (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↓ rectal fullness sensation) is a rectal sensory neuropathy, as in long-standing diabetes. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation; Defecation reflexes; transected spinal cords); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation, rectosphincteric reflex); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Hirschsprung disease). The ↔ cells and the diabetic sensory row are inferred because the sources do not describe them as changed. Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sacral parasympathetic defecation reflex runs from rectal afferents to cord segments S2 to S4 and back through the pelvic nerves (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: spinal cord lesions above the sacral segments leave reflex bowel emptying intact but remove voluntary sphincter control (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerve parasympathetic distribution","isHighYield":true}],"26":[{"id":"sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic α-amylase digests starch into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glucose, galactose, and free fructose units","B":"Maltose, maltotriose, α-limit dextrins","C":"Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose units","D":"Glycogen, amylopectin, and cellulose","E":"Galactose, maltose, and sucrose units"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds in starch and yields three oligosaccharides: α-limit dextrins, maltose, and maltotriose. Brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase, and sucrase then produce glucose. Monosaccharides are the brush-border end products, not amylase products. Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are dietary disaccharides. Glycogen and amylopectin are substrates, and cellulose is not digested. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.27). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: salivary amylase starts starch digestion but is inactivated by low gastric pH (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: human amylase does not hydrolyze the β-1,4 bonds of cellulose (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Brush-border lactase digests lactose into which monosaccharides?","choices":{"A":"Glucose and fructose","B":"Two glucose molecules","C":"Glucose and galactose","D":"Galactose and fructose","E":"Glucose and maltose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lactase splits each lactose into one glucose and one galactose. Glucose plus fructose is the sucrase product, and two glucose molecules come from trehalose or maltose. Galactose with fructose is not a dietary disaccharide product, and maltose is a disaccharide. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose, and trehalase splits trehalose into two glucose molecules (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: about 80 percent of carbohydrate end product is glucose, with galactose and fructose making up the rest (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Undigested lactose in lactase deficiency causes diarrhea by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Active chloride secretion by crypts","B":"Faster colonic motility from gastrin","C":"Mucosal inflammation and blood loss","D":"Bile acid irritation of colonocytes","E":"Osmotic water retention in the lumen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nonabsorbable lactose stays in the lumen and holds an equivalent amount of water to keep contents isosmotic, which causes osmotic diarrhea. Crypt chloride secretion drives secretory diarrhea. Gastrin and motility changes are not the mechanism. Inflammation with blood loss suggests colitis. Bile acid diarrhea follows ileal disease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption, Box 8.2). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic bacteria ferment undigested lactose and produce gas, causing bloating and flatulence (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: lactose intolerance is managed by avoiding milk products or adding lactase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical transporter moves glucose and galactose into intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"GLUT5","B":"GLUT2","C":"GLUT4","D":"SGLT1","E":"NHE3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SGLT1, the Na⁺-glucose cotransporter, carries glucose and galactose across the apical membrane against an electrochemical gradient. GLUT5 is the apical fructose transporter. GLUT2 moves monosaccharides across the basolateral membrane. GLUT4 is the insulin-responsive carrier of muscle and fat. NHE3 is an apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT1 in intestine and SGLT2 in the proximal tubule both use the sodium gradient for glucose uptake (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: glucose and galactose absorption resembles transport in the early proximal convoluted tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"What supplies the energy for glucose uptake by SGLT1?","choices":{"A":"Direct ATP hydrolysis by the SGLT1 protein","B":"Sodium gradient set by the Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","C":"Proton gradient set by apical exchange","D":"Glucose gradient into the enterocyte","E":"Potassium gradient through apical channels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the energy for SGLT1 does not come directly from ATP but from the Na⁺ gradient across the apical membrane, which the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase creates and maintains. This is secondary active transport. Proton gradients drive peptide uptake. Glucose moves against its gradient on SGLT1. Potassium gradients do not drive it. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary active transport couples uphill solute movement to downhill Na⁺ movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Na⁺-glucose cotransport in the small intestine resembles transport in the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"How does fructose cross the apical membrane of intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport via SGLT1","B":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT4","C":"Proton cotransport via PepT1","D":"Primary active ATP pumping","E":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fructose crosses the apical membrane by facilitated diffusion on the fructose-specific transporter GLUT5 and leaves across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2. No cotransporter or energy-requiring step is involved, so fructose moves down its gradient. SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose. GLUT4 is insulin regulated in muscle and fat. PepT1 carries small peptides. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fructose absorption by facilitated diffusion does not proceed against an electrochemical gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: glucose and galactose exit the basolateral membrane on GLUT2, as fructose does (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protease begins protein digestion in the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Elastase","C":"Renin","D":"Pepsin","E":"Thrombin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Protein digestion begins with pepsin, formed when pepsinogen from chief cells meets low gastric pH. Trypsin and elastase are pancreatic proteases that act in the small intestine. Renin is a renal enzyme, and thrombin is a coagulation protease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chief cells secrete pepsinogen, and parietal cells secrete H⁺ that activates it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin is an endopeptidase that digests collagen in meat (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Above what pH is pepsin denatured and inactivated?","choices":{"A":"Above pH 5","B":"Above pH 2","C":"Above pH 3","D":"Above pH 9","E":"Above pH 11"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The three pepsin isozymes have pH optima between 1 and 3, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so it stops acting once chyme is neutralized by duodenal bicarbonate. pH 2 and 3 fall in the optimum range. pH 9 and 11 overshoot the textbook threshold. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic HCO₃⁻ neutralizes duodenal chyme and inactivates pepsin (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin handles about 10 to 20 percent of total protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pancreatic proteases are exopeptidases?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin and chymotrypsin","B":"Pepsin and elastase","C":"Carboxypeptidase A and B","D":"Elastase and trypsin","E":"Enterokinase and pepsin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exopeptidases hydrolyze one amino acid at a time from the C-terminal end; carboxypeptidases A and B are the pancreatic exopeptidases. Trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and pepsin are endopeptidases that cleave interior bonds. Enterokinase is a brush-border activating enzyme. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: brush-border aminopeptidases and dipeptidases finish oligopeptide digestion to absorbable units (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: elastase digests the elastin fibers that hold meat together (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme first converts trypsinogen to trypsin?","choices":{"A":"Aminopeptidase","B":"Chymotrypsin","C":"Elastase","D":"Cathepsin B","E":"Enterokinase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brush-border enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin; trypsin then activates the remaining zymogens and more trypsinogen. Aminopeptidase is a brush-border exopeptidase that trims oligopeptides. Chymotrypsin and elastase are themselves products of trypsin activation. Cathepsin B is a lysosomal protease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins, Fig. 8.29). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enterokinase is also called enteropeptidase (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions). COMLEX: trypsin activates trypsinogen in an autocatalytic step after the first activation (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is enterokinase located?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic zymogen granules","B":"Gastric chief cells","C":"Intestinal brush border","D":"Pancreatic ductal cells","E":"Hepatic bile canaliculi"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme of the small intestinal epithelium, so trypsinogen is first activated in the intestinal lumen, not in the pancreas. Zymogen granules hold inactive precursors. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Ductal cells secrete HCO₃⁻-rich fluid. Bile canaliculi carry bile. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas; Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: separating zymogen storage from the activating enzyme protects the pancreas from autodigestion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin from the first activation step converts further trypsinogen to trypsin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme converts chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and the procarboxypeptidases to active forms?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Enterokinase","C":"Pepsin","D":"Lipase","E":"Amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Once a small amount of trypsin is made by enterokinase, trypsin catalyzes the conversion of the other inactive precursors to chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases A and B. Enterokinase acts on trypsinogen. Pepsin works in the stomach. Lipase and amylase digest fat and starch and do not activate proteases. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absence of trypsin alone looks like loss of the pancreatic proteases because trypsin activates each precursor (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin also activates procolipase and prophospholipase A₂ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"Dipeptides and tripeptides cross the apical membrane by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport","B":"Facilitated diffusion","C":"Primary active transport","D":"Proton cotransport","E":"Receptor endocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Separate H⁺-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into the cell, using an H⁺ gradient created by the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. Sodium cotransport carries free L-amino acids. Facilitated diffusion is the basolateral exit route for amino acids. Primary active transport and endocytosis are not the described routes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins, Fig. 8.31). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide carrier is PepT1 (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: peptide carriers allow protein nutrition despite single amino acid transporter defects (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"In which form is most ingested protein absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Free single amino acids","B":"Dipeptides and tripeptides","C":"Intact dietary protein molecules","D":"Large oligopeptides","E":"Long polypeptide chains"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that most ingested protein is absorbed in the dipeptide and tripeptide forms rather than as free amino acids. Intact proteins and larger oligopeptides are not absorbable and must be broken down by brush-border peptidases. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proteins are absorbable in larger units than carbohydrates, which must reach monosaccharides (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: oligopeptides are hydrolyzed by brush-border proteases before absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to most dipeptides and tripeptides after they enter the epithelial cell?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis by cytoplasmic peptidases","B":"Export back out through apical PepT1","C":"Re-esterification in the smooth ER","D":"Packaging into nascent chylomicrons","E":"Conversion to cellular glycogen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inside the cell, most dipeptides and tripeptides are hydrolyzed to amino acids by cytoplasmic peptidases, and the amino acids exit across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion; a few peptides are absorbed unchanged. PepT1 imports peptides. Re-esterification and chylomicrons belong to lipid absorption. Glycogen storage is unrelated. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal blood receives mostly free amino acids after protein absorption (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: basolateral amino acid exit uses separate carriers for neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has bloating, flatulence, and watery diarrhea within 2 hours of drinking milk. Temperature is 36.9°C, pulse is 78/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. A hydrogen breath test is positive after an oral lactose load. Stool is negative for occult blood. Which stool finding is most likely during symptoms?","choices":{"A":"High stool sodium concentration","B":"Fecal leukocytes and blood","C":"Fat globules on Sudan stain","D":"Diarrhea persisting during fasting","E":"Increased stool osmotic gap"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Undigested lactose holds water osmotically in the lumen, so unmeasured osmoles raise the stool osmotic gap, and the diarrhea stops with fasting. High stool sodium with a low gap fits secretory diarrhea. Leukocytes and blood suggest inflammatory diarrhea. Fat on Sudan stain points to fat malabsorption. Persistent diarrhea while fasting suggests secretion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Box 8.2 lactose intolerance; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial fermentation of lactose raises breath hydrogen and lowers stool pH (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the other brush-border enzymes are preserved in isolated lactase deficiency (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-month-old girl has had watery diarrhea with each formula feeding for 3 weeks after an episode of rotavirus gastroenteritis. Temperature is 37.0°C, and pulse is 128/min. She is mildly dehydrated. Stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 7.0–7.5), and stool reducing substances are positive. Which change in her small intestine best explains this course?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic amylase output","B":"Loss of brush-border lactase","C":"Defective apical glucose cotransport","D":"Absent enterokinase activity","E":"Deficient bile salt secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Viral injury to villus tip cells reduces brush-border lactase, so formula lactose reaches the colon undigested and is fermented, lowering stool pH with positive reducing substances; the deficit recovers as villi regrow. Low amylase affects starch, not lactose. A glucose cotransport defect would be congenital. Enterokinase loss impairs protein digestion. Bile salt deficiency causes fat malabsorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (lactase deficiency, Box 8.2); Guyton Ch. 66 (brush-border disaccharidases). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary lactase deficiency follows mucosal injury from gastroenteritis or celiac disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: lactase sits on the villus brush border, where mucosal injury removes it first (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts acarbose with meals. Two weeks later she reports flatulence and loose stools. Temperature is 36.8°C, and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Hemoglobin A1c is 7.6% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). Inhibition of which digestive step most directly explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border oligosaccharide hydrolysis","B":"Pancreatic triglyceride ester hydrolysis","C":"Basolateral monosaccharide exit","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransport","E":"Gastric pepsin protein hydrolysis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acarbose inhibits brush-border α-glucosidases, so maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins are not split to glucose and reach the colon, where fermentation causes gas and osmotic stool. Triglyceride hydrolysis is the target of orlistat. Basolateral exit and apical cotransport would act on monosaccharides already formed. Pepsin digests protein. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates); First Aid Endocrine (α-glucosidase inhibitors). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: α-glucosidase inhibitors lower postprandial glucose by delaying carbohydrate digestion (First Aid Endocrine, diabetes drugs). COMLEX: oligosaccharides that escape digestion produce osmotic diarrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old boy has severe watery diarrhea with breast milk and with standard formula. Temperature is 36.9°C, pulse is 162/min, and weight is below birth weight. Serum urea nitrogen is 28 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL). Diarrhea stops on a formula whose sole carbohydrate is fructose. Which transporter is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"Apical fructose-specific uniporter","B":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","C":"Brush-border lactase hydrolase","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransporter","E":"Basolateral monosaccharide uniporter"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucose and galactose share the apical SGLT1 carrier, so a defect causes osmotic diarrhea with any glucose or galactose source, while fructose enters through GLUT5 and is tolerated. Loss of the fructose uniporter would spare glucose. A Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase defect would not be selective. Lactase deficiency would tolerate glucose formula. Loss of the basolateral uniporter would block fructose exit too. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucose-galactose malabsorption is an autosomal recessive SGLT1 defect (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: fructose is absorbed independently of sodium (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — solute-coupled water absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has profuse watery diarrhea after travel to an area with a cholera outbreak. Temperature is 37.1°C, pulse is 112/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Serum bicarbonate is 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). An oral rehydration solution corrects his volume deficit. Why does this solution work?","choices":{"A":"Glucose blocks crypt chloride secretion","B":"Sodium enters through the apical GLUT5","C":"Glucose stimulates pancreatic fluid outflow","D":"Sodium uptake no longer requires ATP use","E":"Glucose-coupled sodium uptake is preserved"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholera toxin drives secretion, but apical SGLT1 still couples glucose entry to sodium entry, and water follows the absorbed solute. Glucose does not block chloride secretion. GLUT5 carries fructose, not sodium. Pancreatic fluid adds to losses. Sodium uptake still requires ATP indirectly, because the gradient depends on the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates; oral rehydration in cholera). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: oral rehydration solution uses SGLT1 cotransport of sodium and glucose (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: cholera toxin raises cAMP in crypt cells and opens CFTR chloride channels (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — cholera toxin and secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman training for a marathon tolerates more carbohydrate per hour from a gel that mixes glucose and fructose than from one with glucose alone. Pulse is 58/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Fasting glucose is 84 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). Which property of intestinal absorption best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Fructose activates the glucose cotransporter","B":"Fructose uses a separate apical carrier","C":"Glucose stimulates fructose cotransport","D":"Fructose enters against its gradient","E":"Glucose exits through apical GLUT5"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Glucose uptake is limited by apical SGLT1, while fructose enters through a distinct carrier, GLUT5, by facilitated diffusion, so mixing sugars adds absorptive capacity. Fructose does not activate SGLT1, and it is not cotransported. GLUT5 carries fructose down its gradient. Glucose exits across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLUT2 carries glucose, galactose, and fructose across the basolateral membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: fructose absorption is passive and depends on its concentration gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — monosaccharide transporters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man is seen 1 year after total gastrectomy for gastric adenocarcinoma. Pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg. Serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and fecal nitrogen is normal. Which statement best describes his protein digestion?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal proteases complete digestion","B":"Protein digestion falls by about half","C":"Free amino acid uptake becomes defective","D":"Trypsinogen activation fails in duodenum","E":"Undigested protein reaches the colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pepsin starts protein digestion, but pancreatic and brush-border proteases can complete it, so protein digestion and absorption stay normal after gastrectomy or without gastric acid. Halved digestion overstates pepsin's role. Amino acid transport and enterokinase activation are intestinal and intact. Normal fecal nitrogen argues against colonic protein loss. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: after total gastrectomy, vitamin B₁₂ deficiency follows loss of intrinsic factor (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin accounts for a minor share of protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — intrinsic factor and vitamin B12","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis from alcohol use has bulky stools and weight loss. Temperature is 36.7°C, and pulse is 88/min. Fecal elastase is 80 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g). Fecal nitrogen is increased. Which digestive step is most impaired for dietary protein?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of lactose to monosaccharides","B":"Hydrolysis of proteins to oligopeptides","C":"Activation of pepsinogen in the stomach","D":"Apical uptake of small peptides","E":"Basolateral exit of amino acids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic insufficiency removes trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases, so protein is not broken down to absorbable units and is lost in stool. Lactose hydrolysis is a brush-border step. Pepsinogen activation depends on gastric acid. Peptide uptake and amino acid exit are epithelial transport steps that remain intact. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: low fecal elastase is a marker of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: pancreatic enzyme replacement treats both steatorrhea and creatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has taken high-dose omeprazole for 2 years for reflux esophagitis. Pulse is 70/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Intragastric pH during monitoring is 6.0 (reference range: 1.5–3.5). Which change in gastric protein digestion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin activity rises in the stomach","B":"Trypsinogen stays inactive in the duodenum","C":"Pepsinogen stays inactive in the stomach","D":"Protein absorption ceases in the jejunum","E":"Chief cells stop secreting pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsinogen is converted to pepsin only at low pH, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so gastric proteolysis falls; protein absorption remains normal because pancreatic and brush-border proteases finish the job. Pepsin activity falls, not rises. Trypsinogen activation by enterokinase does not require acid. Chief cells keep secreting. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors irreversibly block the parietal cell H⁺-K⁺ ATPase (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin isozymes have optimal activity between pH 1 and 3 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation and pharmacologic inhibition of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-month-old boy has failure to thrive, diarrhea, and generalized edema. Temperature is 36.8°C, and pulse is 148/min. Serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Sweat chloride is normal. Duodenal fluid has normal trypsinogen content, and adding a brush-border extract to the sample restores proteolysis. Which finding was most likely in the untreated duodenal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic trypsinogen secretion","B":"Low brush-border lactase activity","C":"Low pancreatic α-amylase activity","D":"Low trypsin and elastase activity","E":"Low gastric pepsin enzyme activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Congenital enterokinase deficiency leaves trypsinogen unactivated, and because trypsin activates the other zymogens, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases stay inactive too. Trypsinogen secretion is normal here. Lactase, amylase, and pepsin do not depend on enterokinase. Normal sweat chloride argues against cystic fibrosis. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins; Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypoproteinemic edema reflects low oncotic pressure from protein malabsorption (First Aid Cardiovascular, edema). COMLEX: absence of trypsin mimics loss of the pancreatic proteases (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — pancreatic proteases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back after a weekend of heavy drinking. Temperature is 38.0°C, pulse is 116/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum lipase is 1,450 U/L (reference range: 0–160 U/L). Premature activation of which zymogen inside the pancreas most directly starts the autodigestive cascade?","choices":{"A":"Chymotrypsinogen","B":"Proelastase","C":"Trypsinogen","D":"Procarboxypeptidase A","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Trypsin activates itself and the other pancreatic zymogens, so intrapancreatic trypsinogen activation triggers the cascade of autodigestion. Chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and procarboxypeptidase are downstream targets of trypsin. Pepsinogen is gastric. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, autodigestion; Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: alcohol and gallstones are the leading causes of acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: lipase is more specific than amylase for acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl has had three episodes of acute pancreatitis without gallstones or alcohol use. Temperature is 37.4°C, and pulse is 96/min. Serum triglycerides are 110 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL). Genetic testing shows loss of function of a protein stored with digestive zymogens in acinar cells. Which protective mechanism is most likely lost?","choices":{"A":"Blocking of early active trypsin","B":"Enterokinase storage in acinar cells","C":"Bicarbonate secretion by duct cells","D":"Secretin release from duodenal cells","E":"Bile reflux into the pancreatic duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inhibitors of pancreatic trypsin are copackaged in secretory granules to block trypsin that forms prematurely, so their loss permits autodigestion. Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme, not an acinar protein. Duct bicarbonate and secretin regulate fluid output. Bile reflux is a trigger, not a protection. Normal triglycerides exclude a lipid cause. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, why autodigestion does not occur). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SPINK1 and PRSS1 mutations cause hereditary pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: zymogen granules are impermeable to proteins, keeping enzymes out of the cytoplasm (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy has a photosensitive scaly rash and episodes of ataxia. Pulse is 84/min, and blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Urine shows increased neutral amino acids, and his growth is normal. Serum albumin is 4.3 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which mechanism best explains his normal protein nutrition despite this defect?","choices":{"A":"Absorption on basolateral GLUT2 carriers","B":"Paracellular leak of free amino acids","C":"Pinocytosis of intact dietary protein","D":"Colonic absorption of amino acids","E":"Absorption as dipeptides and tripeptides"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hartnup disease is a defect in the Na⁺-dependent neutral amino acid transporter, but most protein is absorbed as dipeptides and tripeptides on separate H⁺-dependent carriers, so nutrition is preserved; tryptophan loss causes the niacin-deficiency rash. GLUT2 carries monosaccharides. Paracellular leak and pinocytosis are not significant routes. The colon does not absorb amino acids in useful amounts. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins); First Aid Biochemistry (Hartnup disease). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hartnup disease causes pellagra-like findings from tryptophan loss (First Aid Biochemistry). COMLEX: neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids use separate Na⁺ cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — amino acid cotransporters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"In a study of jejunal biopsies from a 41-year-old male volunteer, apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange is blocked with a selective inhibitor. The bath is kept at 37°C, and the dipeptide glycylglycine is added to the luminal side. Uptake of the dipeptide falls sharply, while brush-border peptidase activity is unchanged. What best explains the fall in uptake?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the luminal sodium gradient","B":"Inhibition of brush-border peptidases","C":"Block of basolateral amino acid exit","D":"Loss of the luminal proton gradient","E":"Reduced pancreatic protease release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dipeptide cotransporters use an H⁺ gradient that the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger creates, so blocking the exchanger dissipates the driving force. The sodium gradient drives free amino acid uptake, not peptide uptake. Peptidase activity is unchanged by design. Basolateral exit does not set apical uptake. Biopsies lack pancreatic input. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide transporter uses H⁺ cotransport (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: free amino acid uptake mirrors glucose uptake by Na⁺ cotransport (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man has recurrent flank pain and passes hexagonal urinary crystals. Temperature is 36.9°C, and blood pressure is 120/78 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). His defective transporter also fails to absorb cystine, lysine, and arginine in the intestine. Which transport mechanism normally moves these amino acids into enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Proton-dependent cotransport","B":"Primary active ATP pumping","C":"Sodium-dependent cotransport","D":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis","E":"Paracellular solvent drag"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cystinuria is absence of the Na⁺-amino acid cotransporter for dibasic amino acids in intestine and kidney. Free L-amino acids enter enterocytes by Na⁺-dependent cotransport, like glucose. Proton cotransport carries dipeptides and tripeptides. ATP pumping, endocytosis, and solvent drag are not the described routes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins; Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystinuria causes hexagonal cystine stones with a positive cyanide-nitroprusside test (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: unabsorbed dibasic amino acids are excreted in feces (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption by enterocytes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has bloating and watery diarrhea after drinking juice with added cane sugar, but he tolerates milk. Deficiency of which brush-border enzyme is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Lactase","B":"Trehalase","C":"Amylase","D":"Lipase","E":"Sucrase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose; without it, sucrose stays in the lumen and causes osmotic diarrhea, while lactose is still split by lactase. Lactase deficiency would cause symptoms with milk. Trehalase splits trehalose from mushrooms. Amylase acts on starch, and lipase on fat. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency presents when sucrose enters the diet (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption). COMLEX: sucrase also contributes to digestion of starch oligosaccharides to glucose (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has a normal oral glucose tolerance test. Which transporter carries absorbed glucose out of her enterocytes into the blood?","choices":{"A":"GLUT5","B":"GLUT2","C":"SGLT1","D":"GLUT4","E":"SGLT2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Glucose and galactose leave the enterocyte across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion on GLUT2, which also carries fructose. GLUT5 is the apical fructose carrier. SGLT1 is the apical sodium-coupled entry step. GLUT4 is insulin regulated in muscle and fat. SGLT2 is a renal proximal tubule carrier. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT2 inhibitors lower plasma glucose by blocking renal glucose reabsorption (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: basolateral exit by facilitated diffusion follows the glucose gradient out of the cell (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis takes pancreatic enzyme capsules with meals. Which protease in the capsules digests the elastin fibers of meat?","choices":{"A":"Elastase","B":"Lipase","C":"Amylase","D":"Pepsin","E":"Colipase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Elastase is a pancreatic endopeptidase that hydrolyzes elastin fibers, and it is activated from proelastase by trypsin. Lipase, amylase, and colipase digest fat and starch or assist lipase. Pepsin is gastric, not a pancreatic product. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins); Guyton Ch. 66 (elastase and meat fibers). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency from thick secretions in pancreatic ducts (First Aid Respiratory, cystic fibrosis). COMLEX: endopeptidases cleave interior peptide bonds, unlike exopeptidases (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":3,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman asks why her pancreas does not digest itself. Why are pancreatic proteases secreted as inactive precursors?","choices":{"A":"To speed gastric protein digestion","B":"To allow absorption as intact peptides","C":"To resist bile salt denaturation","D":"To prevent pancreatic autodigestion","E":"To permit storage in pancreatic duct cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancreatic peptidases are stored in zymogen granules as inactive precursors and are activated in the intestinal lumen, which prevents autodigestion of the pancreas. They act in the duodenum, not the stomach. Zymogen form does not change absorption or bile salt resistance. Duct cells secrete bicarbonate, and zymogens are stored in acinar cells. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, why autodigestion does not occur). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: premature intrapancreatic zymogen activation underlies acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: trypsin inhibitors in acinar granules add a second safeguard (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old man eats a high-protein meal. By which mechanism do free amino acids leave his enterocytes into the blood?","choices":{"A":"Facilitated diffusion","B":"Proton cotransport","C":"Sodium cotransport","D":"Primary active pumping","E":"Vesicular exocytosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Free amino acids cross the basolateral membrane into portal blood by facilitated diffusion; the four separate Na⁺-coupled carriers for neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids are the apical entry step. Proton cotransport is the apical route for dipeptides and tripeptides. Sodium cotransport is the apical entry route for free amino acids. Primary active pumping and exocytosis are not described. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins, Fig. 8.31). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: free amino acid entry mirrors glucose entry, with sodium cotransport at the apical membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: absorbed amino acids reach the liver through the portal vein (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman chews bread slowly before swallowing. Why does salivary amylase contribute little to her overall starch digestion?","choices":{"A":"Absence of starch substrate","B":"Rapid absorption by the mouth","C":"Inactivation by gastric acid","D":"Inhibition by bile salts","E":"Need for enterokinase activation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salivary amylase starts starch digestion in the mouth, but it plays little overall role because the low pH of gastric contents inactivates it; pancreatic amylase does most of the work. Bread contains starch. The oral mucosa does not absorb the enzyme. Bile salts and enterokinase do not regulate salivary amylase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic amylase secretion is stimulated by CCK and vagal input (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: salivary amylase activity continues inside the food bolus until gastric acid mixes in (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"grid_sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":4,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman had a total pancreatectomy for an intraductal tumor 6 months ago and has stopped taking her enzyme capsules. She has bulky stools and weight loss. Hemoglobin A1c is 7.9% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). Which of the following sets of carbohydrate digestion findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Lactose digestion","Sucrose digestion","Starch digestion"]},"choices":{"A":["↔","↔","↓"],"B":["↓","↔","↔"],"C":["↔","↓","↔"],"D":["↓","↓","↓"],"E":["↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choice A (↔ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↓ starch digestion) is correct. Without the pancreas there is no pancreatic α-amylase to split starch into maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins, so starch digestion falls; salivary amylase is inactivated by gastric acid and gives little help. Lactose and sucrose are already disaccharides and are split by brush-border lactase and sucrase, which are intact. Choice B (↓ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is lactase deficiency. Choice C (↔ lactose digestion, ↓ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is sucrase-isomaltase deficiency, in which sucrose goes undigested while pancreatic amylase and maltase still handle most starch. Choice D (↓ lactose digestion, ↓ sucrose digestion, ↓ starch digestion) is diffuse villous injury, as in celiac disease, which removes brush-border enzymes. Choice E (↔ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is normal digestion, as with adequate enzyme replacement. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.27; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption syndromes). The ↔ cells, including starch in sucrase-isomaltase deficiency, are inferred because the sources do not grade them. Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetes after total pancreatectomy reflects loss of islet insulin and glucagon (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase, and sucrase finish starch digestion to glucose (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true}],"27":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes each triglyceride molecule into which products?","choices":{"A":"One monoglyceride and two fatty acids","B":"Glycerol and three free fatty acids","C":"Two monoglycerides and one fatty acid","D":"Cholesterol and two free fatty acids","E":"Lysolecithin and one free fatty acid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic lipase, secreted in active form, splits a triglyceride into one monoglyceride and two fatty acids. Pancreatic lipase does not release free glycerol, and two monoglycerides would need a second glycerol backbone. Cholesterol ester hydrolase splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids, and lysolecithin comes from phospholipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lingual and gastric lipases hydrolyze about 10 percent of ingested triglyceride before the chyme reaches the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the optimum pH for pancreatic lipase is about 6, so duodenal acid must be neutralized by bicarbonate (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main function of colipase in lipid digestion?","choices":{"A":"Emulsifying lipid droplets in the gastric lumen","B":"Activating trypsinogen in the duodenal lumen","C":"Displacing bile salts from lipase","D":"Hydrolyzing cholesterol esters to free cholesterol","E":"Packaging lipids into chylomicrons in enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts displace pancreatic lipase from emulsified droplets. Colipase, activated from procolipase by trypsin, displaces the bile salts and binds lipase so digestion can proceed. Dietary proteins emulsify fat in the stomach, enterokinase activates trypsinogen, cholesterol ester hydrolase acts on cholesterol esters, and enterocytes assemble chylomicrons. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: CCK released by fat in the duodenum slows gastric emptying and gives pancreatic enzymes time to act (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic phospholipase A2 hydrolyzes dietary phospholipids into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glycerol and fatty acids","B":"Cholesterol and fatty acids","C":"Monoglyceride and fatty acids","D":"Lysolecithin and fatty acids","E":"Bile acids and free glycine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phospholipase A2, secreted as a proenzyme and activated by trypsin, converts phospholipids to lysolecithin and fatty acids. Lysolecithin then joins bile salts in emulsifying fat. The other pairs come from triglyceride or cholesterol ester hydrolysis, and bile acids with glycine come from deconjugation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile salts are amphipathic, so the hydrophilic side faces water and the hydrophobic side faces the lipid core (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the end products of lipid digestion, other than glycerol, are insoluble in water and must be carried in micelles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"In which organelle do enterocytes re-esterify absorbed lipid digestion products?","choices":{"A":"Rough endoplasmic reticulum","B":"Golgi secretory apparatus","C":"Apical brush-border membrane","D":"Lysosomal digestive compartment","E":"Smooth endoplasmic reticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inside the enterocyte, monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, and lysolecithin are re-esterified on the smooth endoplasmic reticulum to triglycerides, cholesterol esters, and phospholipids. The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles afterward. Lipids cross the brush border by diffusion, and lysosomes digest heme, not lipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chylomicrons carry dietary triglyceride and are cleared by lipoprotein lipase in peripheral capillaries (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: lipid crosses the apical membrane by diffusion, so absorption depends on mucosal surface area (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"Chylomicrons leave the intestinal villus by entering which vessels?","choices":{"A":"Portal venules","B":"Villus blood capillaries","C":"Hepatic sinusoids","D":"Lymphatic lacteals","E":"Mesenteric arterioles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chylomicrons are about 1000 Å across, too large to enter vascular capillaries, so they move between endothelial cells into lacteals and reach the blood through the thoracic duct. Portal venules and villus capillaries carry water-soluble nutrients. Hepatic sinusoids and arterioles are not the exit route. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: injury to the thoracic duct leaks chylomicron-rich lymph, giving milky fluid high in triglyceride (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: glycerol is water soluble and does not need micelles for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to the bile salts of a micelle when its lipids are absorbed at the brush border?","choices":{"A":"They enter the enterocyte with the lipids","B":"They are packaged into chylomicrons","C":"They remain in the intestinal lumen","D":"They are re-esterified in the cytosol","E":"They are degraded by brush-border enzymes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Micelles release lipids at the apical membrane, but the micelles themselves do not enter the cell. The bile salts stay in the lumen and are absorbed downstream in the ileum. They are not part of chylomicrons, not re-esterified, and not degraded at the brush border. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed micelles are disks about 50 Å across with bile salts lining the outside (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile salt reuse through the enterohepatic circulation keeps the pool large without heavy hepatic synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"In which intestinal segment are conjugated bile salts actively reabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Proximal jejunum","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Gastric antrum","E":"Terminal ileum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Conjugated bile salts are reabsorbed in the ileum and returned to the liver in portal blood, the enterohepatic circulation. About 95 percent of secreted bile acids return this way. The duodenum and jejunum are where bile salts work on lipids. The colon and stomach do not reabsorb them. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile; Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 7α-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: Crohn disease of the terminal ileum can interrupt bile salt reabsorption and cause fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does surgical removal of the intestinal segment that reabsorbs bile salts shrink the total bile salt pool?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic synthesis stops after resection","B":"Hepatic synthesis lags behind fecal loss","C":"Bile salts are deconjugated in the bile","D":"Gallbladder storage is lost to the colon","E":"Micelles enter the ileal wall intact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Resecting the reabsorptive segment interrupts the enterohepatic circulation, so bile salts go out in feces. The liver increases synthesis, but synthesis falls short of the fecal loss and the pool shrinks. Synthesis rises rather than stopping. Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism. Gallbladder storage is unaffected, and micelles do not enter cells. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption from short bowel or Crohn disease can cause fat malabsorption and bile acid diarrhea (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: a smaller bile salt pool limits micelle formation and causes steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"By which intestinal level are most ingested lipids absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Mid-jejunum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Gastric antrum","D":"Proximal colon","E":"Rectosigmoid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most dietary lipid is absorbed by the mid-jejunum, so the work of bile salts is done long before they reach the terminal ileum for reabsorption. The ileum recycles bile salts rather than absorbing most fat, and the stomach and colon absorb little lipid. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: short bowel syndrome follows extensive small bowel resection and causes malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, short bowel syndrome). COMLEX: bile salts left in the lumen after lipid uptake travel on to the ileum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cells secrete intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Gastric chief cells","B":"Pancreatic acinar cells","C":"Gastric parietal cells","D":"Salivary gland cells","E":"Duodenal S cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intrinsic factor is a glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells, the same cells that secrete H+. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, acinar cells secrete pancreatic enzymes, salivary glands secrete R proteins, and S cells secrete secretin. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune destruction of parietal cells causes chronic gastritis and pernicious anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: after gastrectomy, oral B12 is not absorbed and B12 must be given by injection (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Free vitamin B12 released in the stomach first binds to which carrier?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"R protein","C":"Transferrin","D":"Apoferritin","E":"Calbindin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Free B12 binds R proteins from salivary secretions. In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins and B12 moves to intrinsic factor. Transferrin and apoferritin carry iron, and calbindin binds calcium. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency can impair B12 absorption as well as fat absorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: most water-soluble vitamins use sodium-dependent cotransport, unlike B12 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"What event in the duodenum allows vitamin B12 to bind intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic proteases","B":"Gastric hydrochloric acid","C":"Conjugated bile salts","D":"Brush-border lactase","E":"Salivary amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins, so B12 transfers to intrinsic factor. The B12-intrinsic factor complex resists these proteases and travels to the ileum. Acid and pepsin act earlier in the stomach, and bile salts, lactase, and amylase do not free B12. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia with neurologic findings (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Active vitamin D increases intestinal calcium absorption mainly by inducing which protein?","choices":{"A":"Apoferritin storage protein","B":"Transferrin carrier","C":"Pancreatic colipase","D":"Calbindin D-28K","E":"Intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol induces the vitamin D-dependent calcium-binding protein calbindin D-28K in enterocytes, which promotes calcium absorption. Apoferritin and transferrin handle iron, colipase helps lipase, and intrinsic factor binds B12. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inadequate calcium absorption causes rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: 25-hydroxycholecalciferol is the main circulating but inactive form of vitamin D (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How do absorbed vitamins A, D, E, and K leave the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Bound to albumin in portal blood","B":"By sodium cotransport into blood","C":"In micelles across the basal membrane","D":"Bound to transferrin in portal blood","E":"In chylomicrons into lymph"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fat-soluble vitamins are handled like dietary lipids. They travel in micelles, diffuse into the cell, are incorporated into chylomicrons, and are extruded into lymph. Sodium cotransport handles most water-soluble vitamins, micelles do not enter the cell, and transferrin carries iron. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat inhibits gastric and pancreatic lipase and decreases absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How is heme iron converted to free iron inside the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Digestion by pancreatic proteases","B":"Digestion by lysosomal enzymes","C":"Hydrolysis by brush-border peptidases","D":"Cleavage by gastric pepsin","E":"Release by bile salt micelles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Heme iron crosses the apical membrane intact. Inside the enterocyte, lysosomal enzymes digest heme and release free iron, which binds apoferritin and then crosses the basolateral membrane. The other enzymes act in the lumen, and micelles carry lipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis increases intestinal iron absorption through low hepcidin (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: transferrin carries absorbed iron to the liver and bone marrow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman starts a weight-loss drug that blocks gastric and pancreatic lipase. One month later she has oily, foul-smelling stools. Temperature is 36.9°C, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Stool fat is 14 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which digestive step is most directly impaired?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of dietary triglyceride","B":"Exocytosis of chylomicrons into lymph","C":"Synthesis of apolipoprotein B in enterocytes","D":"Reabsorption of bile salts in the ileum","E":"Activation of procolipase by trypsin enzyme"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Blocking lipase leaves triglycerides unhydrolyzed. Undigested triglycerides are not absorbable and appear in the stool. Chylomicron export and apoprotein synthesis follow digestion. Ileal bile salt reabsorption and procolipase activation are not drug targets here. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (orlistat). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat causes steatorrhea and decreased absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: lingual and gastric lipases normally digest only about 10 percent of triglyceride (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has recurrent duodenal ulcers, diarrhea, and greasy stools. Fasting gastrin is 1200 pg/mL (reference range: <100 pg/mL), and duodenal aspirate pH is 3 (reference range: 6–7). Exocrine enzyme output is normal. Which mechanism best explains his fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Loss of hepatic bile salt secretion","B":"Defective chylomicron assembly in enterocytes","C":"Excess colipase release from acini","D":"Reduced ileal absorptive surface","E":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A gastrin-secreting tumor drives excess H+ secretion that overwhelms pancreatic bicarbonate. Duodenal pH stays far below the lipase optimum of about 6, so pancreatic lipase is inactivated. Bile secretion, chylomicron assembly, and ileal surface are intact, and colipase helps lipase rather than causing malabsorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a gastrin-secreting tumor, often of the pancreas or duodenum (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: a proton pump inhibitor reduces the H+ load delivered to the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and gastrin","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has jaundice, dark urine, and pale bulky stools. Imaging shows a mass blocking the common bile duct. Total bilirubin is 9.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.2 mg/dL). Pancreatic enzyme secretion is normal. Which mechanism best explains his steatorrhea?","choices":{"A":"Loss of pancreatic lipase activity in the duodenal lumen","B":"Failure of trypsin to activate procolipase","C":"Failure of emulsification and micelle formation","D":"Defective apoprotein synthesis in enterocytes","E":"Obstruction of intestinal lymphatic drainage"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts emulsify fat and form the mixed micelles that carry lipid products to the brush border. When bile fails to reach the duodenum, lipid digestion and micellar transport fail. Pancreatic lipase, colipase activation, apoprotein synthesis, and lymph flow are intact. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pale stools and dark urine point to obstructed bile flow with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: bile salts are amphipathic, which lets them line micelles in the aqueous lumen (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile formation and micelles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-month-old boy has failure to thrive and pale, foul-smelling stools. Blood smear shows acanthocytes. Fasting triglyceride is 8 mg/dL (reference range: 30–150 mg/dL), and duodenal biopsy shows enterocytes filled with lipid droplets. Which defect best explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Failure of micelle formation","B":"Inactive pancreatic lipase","C":"Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption","D":"Failure of chylomicron assembly","E":"Deficient brush-border lactase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Failure to synthesize Apo B causes abetalipoproteinemia. Chylomicrons do not form or are not exported to lymph, so lipid builds up in enterocytes and plasma lipid stays low. Lipid filling the cells shows that micelle formation, lipase digestion, and uptake into the cell are working. Bile salt uptake and lactase do not explain lipid-filled enterocytes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia presents with acanthocytes, steatorrhea, and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: apoproteins cover about 20 percent of the chylomicron surface (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 5 — abetalipoproteinemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man develops a milky right pleural effusion 5 days after esophagectomy. Pleural fluid triglyceride is 420 mg/dL (reference range: <110 mg/dL). He is placed on a diet in which medium-chain triglycerides replace long-chain fats. Why does this diet reduce the leak?","choices":{"A":"Medium-chain fats enter portal blood directly","B":"Medium-chain fats bypass bile salt emulsification","C":"Medium-chain fats increase lacteal reabsorption","D":"Medium-chain fats block chylomicron uptake by tissues","E":"Medium-chain fats seal the thoracic duct wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long-chain fats are re-esterified and packaged in chylomicrons, which travel in lacteals to the thoracic duct. Small amounts of short- and medium-chain fatty acids are more water soluble and diffuse straight into portal blood, so less lipid enters the injured duct. The other options are not mechanisms of this diet. Bypassing bile salt emulsification is not why the leak falls; medium-chain fats diffuse into portal capillaries rather than entering lymph. Medium-chain fats do not increase lacteal reabsorption; they avoid lacteals altogether by diffusing across the basolateral membrane into portal capillaries. Medium-chain fats do not block chylomicron uptake; they simply bypass re-esterification and chylomicron packaging and enter portal blood. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Guyton Ch. 66 (Direct absorption of fatty acids into the portal blood). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thoracic duct carries intestinal lymph and chylomicrons into the left subclavian vein (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: chylomicrons are too large to enter vascular capillaries (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus lacteals","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Bypassing bile salt emulsification is not why the leak falls","C":"Medium-chain fats do not increase lacteal reabsorption; they","D":"Medium-chain fats do not block chylomicron uptake; they simp"}},{"id":"sdl27_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools. Tissue transglutaminase IgA is positive, and duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy. Hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Pancreatic function is normal. Which mechanism best explains her impaired fat absorption?","choices":{"A":"Absent pancreatic lipase secretion into the duodenum","B":"Reduced mucosal surface for lipid diffusion","C":"Deficient apoprotein B synthesis in enterocytes","D":"Loss of bile salt conjugation","E":"Blocked lacteal drainage by tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lipid products leave micelles and cross the apical membrane by diffusion, which depends on surface area. Villous loss reduces that area and impairs fat absorption. Lipase secretion is normal, apoprotein synthesis is not the lesion, bile salt conjugation is hepatic, and no lymphatic tumor is present. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease mainly affects the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum and is linked to iron deficiency anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease gives an abnormal D-xylose test, unlike pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with Crohn disease had 100 cm of terminal ileum resected. She now has steatorrhea and flank pain, and imaging shows a renal stone. Stone analysis shows a calcium stone. Serum calcium is 9.1 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains her kidney stone?","choices":{"A":"Increased colonic oxalate absorption","B":"Increased hepatic bile acid synthesis","C":"Decreased renal calcium filtration","D":"Increased intestinal calcium uptake","E":"Decreased colonic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lost bile salts shrink the pool, so fat is malabsorbed. Unabsorbed fatty acids bind luminal calcium, leaving oxalate free to be absorbed in the colon and excreted in urine as calcium oxalate stones. Increased hepatic synthesis falls short but does not form stones. Calcium uptake falls rather than rises. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fat malabsorption from ileal bile salt loss causes calcium to bind fat, so oxalate is absorbed and forms calcium oxalate stones (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: about 95 percent of secreted bile acids normally return to the liver (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man had 30 cm of terminal ileum removed after a penetrating injury. He now has watery diarrhea several times daily without weight loss, and it improves with cholestyramine. Stool fat is 6 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which mechanism best explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Undigested lactose holding water in the lumen","B":"Gastrin-driven acid overload of the duodenum","C":"Bile acids stimulating colonic secretion","D":"Bacterial deconjugation in the jejunum","E":"Loss of colonic surface for water uptake"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A short ileal resection lets bile acids spill into the colon, where they stimulate secretion and cause watery diarrhea. The liver still makes enough bile acid to keep micelles adequate, so stool fat stays near normal. A binding resin removes the irritant. Lactose, gastrin, deconjugation, and colonic loss are not the mechanism here. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption can cause bile acid diarrhea as well as fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: longer ileal resections deplete the pool faster than the liver can replace it, adding steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a surgical blind loop of small bowel has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools. A hydrogen breath test is positive soon after a glucose load. Vitamin B12 is 160 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which mechanism best explains her fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase","B":"Loss of the ileal bile salt carrier","C":"Failure of chylomicron export to lymph","D":"Bacterial deconjugation of bile salts","E":"Autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bacteria remove glycine and taurine from bile salts, making nonionized bile acids that are absorbed too early, before micelles finish their work. The pool reaching the lumen falls and fat absorption fails. Acid inactivation follows gastrinoma, carrier loss follows ileal disease, chylomicron failure is abetalipoproteinemia, and intrinsic factor loss does not cause steatorrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial overgrowth can cause B12 deficiency because bacteria consume the vitamin (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: low luminal pH also promotes early bile acid absorption by converting bile acids to the nonionized form (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has fatigue and numbness in both feet. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), and mean corpuscular volume is 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Antibodies to parietal cells are positive. Which route of vitamin B12 replacement is most reliable for her?","choices":{"A":"Oral tablets taken with food","B":"Oral tablets with antacid","C":"Sublingual tablets at bedtime","D":"Oral liquid with pancreatic enzymes","E":"Intramuscular injection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Autoimmune loss of parietal cells removes intrinsic factor, so dietary or oral B12 is not absorbed in the ileum. Costanzo states that B12 must be given by injection in this setting. Antacids and pancreatic enzymes do not replace intrinsic factor. Sublingual tablets still deliver B12 by a non-injected route, and without intrinsic factor parenteral intramuscular B12 is required to bypass the intestinal pathway. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pernicious anemia reflects autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor from parietal cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: B12 deficiency also causes neurologic findings, which folate does not correct (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Sublingual tablets still deliver B12 by a non-injected route"}},{"id":"sdl27_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with Crohn disease had 90 cm of terminal ileum resected 4 years ago. He now has fatigue and paresthesias. Mean corpuscular volume is 114 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and serum B12 is 110 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Gastric biopsy is normal. Which step of B12 absorption is lost?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food by pepsin","B":"Ileal uptake of the B12-intrinsic factor complex","C":"Binding of free B12 to R proteins","D":"Gastric secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","E":"Protease cleavage of R proteins in the duodenum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The B12-intrinsic factor complex is absorbed by a specific transport mechanism in the ileum. Removing the terminal ileum removes that site even though the stomach makes intrinsic factor normally. Pepsin release, R protein binding, intrinsic factor secretion, and protease cleavage all occur above the ileum. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: body B12 stores last years, so deficiency appears long after resection (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with chronic alcoholic pancreatitis has greasy stools and weight loss. Mean corpuscular volume is 106 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and serum B12 is 170 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Gastric biopsy is normal. Which step of B12 absorption is most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food in the stomach","B":"Secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","C":"Uptake of the complex in the terminal ileum","D":"Degradation of R proteins in the duodenum","E":"Binding of B12 to transferrin in plasma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins in the duodenum so B12 can transfer to intrinsic factor. Without them, B12 stays on R proteins and is poorly absorbed. Pepsin release and parietal cell function are normal, the ileum is intact, and transferrin carries iron. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat, fat-soluble vitamins, and vitamin B12 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal D-xylose test because the mucosa is intact (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man on hemodialysis has bone pain and muscle cramps. Serum calcium is 7.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL), and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. Which mechanism best explains his low intestinal calcium absorption?","choices":{"A":"Deficient hepatic 25-hydroxylation","B":"Deficient renal 1α-hydroxylation","C":"Excess intestinal transferrin synthesis","D":"Loss of intrinsic factor secretion","E":"Impaired chylomicron formation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The proximal tubule converts 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to active 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol through 1α-hydroxylase. In chronic kidney disease this step fails, calbindin D-28K induction falls, and calcium absorption drops. Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is intact because 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. The other choices involve iron, B12, and lipid. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults, inadequate calcium absorption causes osteomalacia (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: active vitamin D acts on intestine, kidney, and bone (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — calcium absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has fatigue, joint pain, and darkening skin. Transferrin saturation is 78% (reference range: 20–50%), and ferritin is 1900 ng/mL (reference range: 24–336 ng/mL). Genetic testing shows a homozygous HFE mutation. Which change best explains his iron overload?","choices":{"A":"High hepcidin increasing iron absorption","B":"Loss of lysosomal heme digestion","C":"Excess intrinsic factor secretion","D":"Excess vitamin D-induced calbindin","E":"Low hepcidin increasing iron absorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Absorbed iron moves from apoferritin across the basolateral membrane and onto transferrin. HFE mutation lowers hepcidin, which normally limits that export, so absorption runs high and iron accumulates. High hepcidin would decrease absorption. Lysosomal heme digestion, intrinsic factor, and calbindin are unrelated. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron); First Aid Hematology (hemochromatosis). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis is linked to HFE mutation on chromosome 6 (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: iron enters enterocytes as Fe2+ or as heme iron (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — iron absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has stopped taking her pancreatic enzyme capsules. She now has easy bruising and greasy stools. Prothrombin time is 19 seconds (reference range: 11–13.5 seconds), and platelet count is normal. Deficiency of which vitamin best explains her bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin C","B":"Vitamin B12","C":"Vitamin K","D":"Vitamin B1","E":"Folic acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Without pancreatic lipase, fat is not digested and fat-soluble vitamins, which travel with lipids in micelles and chylomicrons, are malabsorbed. Vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time. Vitamin C, B1, and folate are water-soluble vitamins absorbed by sodium-dependent cotransport. B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption syndromes). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency from cystic fibrosis causes malabsorption of vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: vitamin K is needed to make clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — malabsorption and pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-week-old infant digests some milk fat before it reaches the duodenum. Which enzymes start this digestion?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic phospholipase A2","B":"Brush-border disaccharidases","C":"Hepatic lipoprotein lipase","D":"Lingual and gastric lipases","E":"Salivary α-amylase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lingual and gastric lipases hydrolyze about 10 percent of ingested triglyceride before chyme reaches the small intestine. Phospholipase A2 is pancreatic, disaccharidases act on sugars, lipoprotein lipase clears plasma lipoproteins, and amylase digests starch. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the stomach, dietary proteins keep lipid droplets emulsified because bile acids are absent (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: slow gastric emptying gives pancreatic enzymes time to digest lipid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old man eats a fatty meal. Which molecules cover most of the surface of the chylomicrons his enterocytes export?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipids","B":"Triglycerides","C":"Cholesterol esters","D":"Bile salts","E":"Free fatty acids"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Phospholipids cover about 80 percent of the chylomicron surface, and apoproteins cover the rest. Triglycerides and cholesterol esters form the core. Bile salts stay in the lumen, and free fatty acids are re-esterified. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Apo B is required for chylomicron formation (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: chylomicrons reach the blood through the thoracic duct (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman's liver must replace bile acids lost in her stool. Which enzyme limits the rate of new bile acid synthesis?","choices":{"A":"HMG-CoA reductase","B":"Lipoprotein lipase","C":"Pancreatic colipase","D":"Phospholipase A2","E":"Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis from cholesterol. HMG-CoA reductase limits cholesterol synthesis, lipoprotein lipase clears plasma lipoproteins, and colipase and phospholipase A2 act in the intestinal lumen. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid loss in feces increases hepatic bile acid synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile acids are conjugated with glycine or taurine before secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man eats meat as his main source of vitamin B12. What releases the vitamin from food proteins in his stomach?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"Lysosomal enzymes","C":"Pepsin digestion","D":"Bile salts","E":"Brush-border peptidases"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dietary B12 is released from food by pepsin in the stomach, then binds R proteins. Intrinsic factor binds B12 later in the duodenum, lysosomal enzymes act inside cells, bile salts form micelles, and brush-border peptidases act in the small intestine. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 is found in animal-derived foods, so strict vegans risk deficiency (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: R proteins come from salivary secretions (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman takes oral iron for anemia. Which plasma protein carries the absorbed iron to her liver and marrow?","choices":{"A":"Apoferritin","B":"Transferrin","C":"Albumin","D":"Calbindin","E":"Ceruloplasmin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transferrin, a β-globulin, carries iron from the intestine to liver stores and then to bone marrow. Apoferritin binds iron inside enterocytes, albumin is not the iron carrier, calbindin binds calcium, and ceruloplasmin carries copper. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: low hepcidin in hemochromatosis increases iron absorption (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: heme iron is absorbed intact and freed inside the enterocyte (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man eats eggs rich in sterol esters. Which pancreatic enzyme frees the sterol for micelles?","choices":{"A":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase","B":"Phospholipase A2","C":"Pancreatic amylase","D":"Carboxypeptidase A","E":"Enteropeptidase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase is secreted in active form and splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids. Phospholipase A2 acts on phospholipids, amylase on starch, carboxypeptidase on peptides, and enteropeptidase on trypsinogen. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: free cholesterol is solubilized in mixed micelles for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cholesterol ester hydrolase can also release glycerol from triglycerides (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl27_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":4,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with high LDL cholesterol starts cholestyramine, which binds bile acids in the intestinal lumen. Months later he has mild bloating. LDL cholesterol is 182 mg/dL (reference range: <130 mg/dL) before treatment. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely after treatment begins?","grid":{"headers":["Fecal bile acids","Hepatic bile acid synthesis","Vitamin K absorption"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↑","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↓"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↓"],"E":["↓","↓","↑"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choice B (↑ fecal bile acids, ↑ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) is correct. Bound bile acids escape ileal reabsorption and leave in stool, interrupting the enterohepatic circulation. The liver raises synthesis to replace the loss, and a smaller luminal bile acid pool reduces micelle formation, so absorption of fat-soluble vitamin K falls. Choice A (↓ fecal bile acids, ↑ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) has the fecal loss backward; a binder increases, not decreases, stool bile acids. Choice C (↑ fecal bile acids, ↓ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) ignores the hepatic response to bile acid loss, in which reduced FXR feedback derepresses synthesis. Choice D (↔ fecal bile acids, ↔ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) fits a lipase inhibitor such as orlistat rather than a bile acid binder. Choice E (↓ fecal bile acids, ↓ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↑ vitamin K absorption) reverses every effect, as if bile acids were supplemented. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile; Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption; Fat-soluble vitamins). The vitamin cell is inferred from micelle dependence; the sources do not grade it for this drug. Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after ileal resection, hepatic synthesis rises but does not keep pace with fecal bile salt loss (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile acid loss in Crohn disease of the ileum can cause calcium oxalate kidney stones (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true}],"28":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"About how much fluid enters the gastrointestinal lumen each day from diet plus secretions?","choices":{"A":"About 2 L","B":"About 4 L","C":"About 15 L","D":"About 9 L","E":"About 0.5 L"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 2 L of dietary fluid plus about 7 L of salivary, gastric, pancreatic, biliary, and intestinal secretions gives slightly more than 9 L. Most is absorbed by the small intestine and colon. 2 L is the dietary share alone. About 4 L underestimates the load; ingested plus secreted fluid totals roughly 9 L per day. About 15 L overstates normal daily load; volumes above 10–14 L per day are reached only in cholera-type secretory diarrhea. About 0.5 L is far below the roughly 9 L of ingested plus secreted fluid the intestine handles each day. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 9 L absorbed daily is almost equal to the whole extracellular fluid volume, so diarrhea can rapidly deplete it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: crypt cells add secreted fluid that villus cells then reabsorb (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"About 4 L underestimates the load; ingested plus secreted fl","C":"About 15 L overstates normal daily load; volumes above 10–14","E":"About 0.5 L is far below the roughly 9 L of ingested plus se"}},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the osmolarity of the fluid absorbed by the intestine compared with plasma?","choices":{"A":"Hyposmotic to plasma","B":"Isosmotic to plasma","C":"Hyperosmotic to plasma","D":"Variable with meal tonicity","E":"Hyperosmotic in the jejunum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The absorbate is isosmotic: solute and water are absorbed in proportion, as in the renal proximal tubule. It is not hyposmotic or hyperosmotic, and it does not vary with meal tonicity or segment. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: jejunal transport mechanisms resemble those of the early renal proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the small remaining volume of 100–200 mL is lost in feces each day (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first step in intestinal fluid absorption by villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of solute","B":"Absorption of water","C":"Secretion of chloride","D":"Secretion of potassium","E":"Secretion of mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Villus cells first absorb solute, and water follows osmotically so the absorbate stays isosmotic. Water does not move first. Chloride secretion is a crypt function, potassium secretion occurs in the colon, and mucus does not drive fluid uptake. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cell type determines direction, with villus cells absorbing and crypt cells secreting fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: disease that reduces absorptive surface area, such as small intestinal infection, reduces fluid absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"The jejunum mainly produces net absorption of which salt?","choices":{"A":"Sodium chloride","B":"Potassium chloride","C":"Sodium bicarbonate","D":"Potassium bicarbonate","E":"Calcium chloride"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the jejunum, H+ leaves on apical Na+-H+ exchange and HCO3- made by carbonic anhydrase is absorbed into blood, giving net NaHCO3 absorption. The ileum adds Cl--HCO3- exchange and absorbs net NaCl. The colon absorbs Na+ and secretes K+. Potassium chloride is not the jejunal absorbate; the colon handles K⁺, secreting it through apical K⁺ channels. Potassium bicarbonate is not absorbed in the jejunum; H⁺ is exchanged for Na⁺, so the cation absorbed with HCO₃⁻ is sodium. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the jejunum is the major site of Na+ absorption in the small intestine (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: jejunal Na+ also enters on Na+-glucose, Na+-galactose, and Na+-amino acid cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Potassium chloride is not the jejunal absorbate; the colon h","D":"Potassium bicarbonate is not absorbed in the jejunum; H⁺ is "}},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone induces synthesis of apical Na⁺ channels in colonic epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Antidiuretic hormone","B":"Natriuretic peptide","C":"Cholecystokinin","D":"Aldosterone","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Like renal principal cells, colonocytes have apical Na+ and K+ channels, and aldosterone induces Na+ channel synthesis. This increases Na+ absorption and, secondarily, K+ secretion. ADH acts on renal water channels, ANP promotes natriuresis, and CCK and secretin regulate pancreatic and biliary secretion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic cells resemble the principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: increased Na+ entry raises basolateral pumping, which drives K+ into the cell for secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical exchanger is present in the ileum but not the jejunum?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"H⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ileum has the jejunal mechanisms plus apical Cl--HCO3- exchange and a basolateral Cl- transporter. Combined with Na+-H+ exchange, this gives net NaCl absorption. Na+-H+ exchange and Na+-glucose cotransport are present in both segments. The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is basolateral in crypt cells, and H+-K+ ATPase is gastric. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the ileum, HCO3- is secreted into the lumen rather than absorbed into blood (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the ileal basolateral membrane has a Cl- transporter instead of an HCO3- transporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal cells secrete chloride and water into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Villus tip enterocytes","B":"Goblet mucus cells","C":"Paneth defensin cells","D":"Enteroendocrine I cells","E":"Crypt epithelial cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Epithelial cells lining the crypts secrete fluid and electrolytes, while cells lining the villi absorb them. Goblet cells secrete mucus, Paneth cells secrete defensins, and I cells secrete CCK. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: crypts of Lieberkühn contain stem cells that replace enterocytes and goblet cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, digestive tract histology). COMLEX: normally villus cells reabsorb what crypt cells secrete (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which basolateral transporter brings chloride from the blood into chloride-secreting intestinal cells?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger protein","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"Basolateral K⁺ leak channel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crypt cells have a basolateral Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter like the one in the thick ascending limb. Cl- enters on it and exits through apical Cl- channels. Na+-glucose cotransport and Na+-H+ exchange are apical absorptive carriers, and Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal mechanism. The basolateral K⁺ leak channel only recycles K⁺ that NKCC1 brings in; it is not the carrier that loads Cl⁻ into crypt cells. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the same three-ion cotransporter in the thick ascending limb is the target of loop diuretics (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: the Na+-K+ ATPase on the basolateral membrane keeps intracellular Na+ low for the cotransporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The basolateral K⁺ leak channel only recycles K⁺ that NKCC1 "}},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"How does sodium reach the lumen during intestinal chloride secretion?","choices":{"A":"Passively by the paracellular route","B":"Through apical Na⁺ channels into the lumen","C":"On the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","D":"By apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","E":"By vesicular transcytosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cl- is secreted through apical channels, and Na+ follows passively by the paracellular route between cells. Water then follows the NaCl. Apical Na+ channels and Na+-H+ exchange move Na+ into cells, and the Na+-K+ ATPase is basolateral. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leaky small-intestinal tight junctions permit substantial paracellular movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon has high-resistance tight junctions that limit paracellular flow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which covalent change does cholera toxin make to the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Tyrosine phosphorylation","B":"Glycosylation","C":"Ubiquitination","D":"Methylation","E":"ADP-ribosylation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The A subunit of cholera toxin catalyzes ADP-ribosylation of the αs subunit of the Gs protein. The other modifications are not the toxin's action. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates the αs subunit so Gs stays GTP-bound and adenylyl cyclase stays active (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane and acts on Gs at the basolateral side (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the direct effect of cholera toxin on the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Loss of its GTP binding","B":"Faster GDP rebinding","C":"Loss of its GTPase activity","D":"Faster receptor desensitizing","E":"Gain of Gi-type coupling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ADP-ribosylation inhibits the GTPase activity of αs, so GTP stays bound and the subunit stays active. GTP binding is kept, not lost. Faster GDP rebinding would end the signal, and the toxin does not change receptor desensitization or coupling. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: with GTP permanently bound, cAMP stays high and apical Cl- channels stay open (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the secreted fluid volume can reach 10 to 12 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Why does cholera cause massive diarrhea despite intact villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Villus cells switch over to net fluid secretion","B":"Crypt secretion exceeds villus absorption","C":"Colonic water absorption is abolished","D":"Toxin destroys the villus epithelium","E":"Bacteria invade the submucosal layer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholera stimulates crypt Cl- and fluid secretion so strongly that it overwhelms the absorptive capacity of villus cells and the colon. Villus cells keep absorbing, which is why glucose-coupled Na+ absorption still works. The toxin does not destroy the epithelium or cause invasion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vibrio cholerae and some Escherichia coli strains cause secretory diarrhea through toxins (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon can reabsorb a maximum of about 6 to 8 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Which laxative class includes lactulose and polyethylene glycol?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic laxatives","B":"Stimulant laxatives","C":"Bulk-forming laxatives","D":"Emollient laxatives","E":"Opioid antidiarrheals"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osmotic laxatives provide a nonabsorbable osmotic load that draws water into the lumen, the mechanism of osmotic diarrhea. Stimulant laxatives act on enteric nerves, bulk-forming agents are soluble fibers, emollients lower stool surface tension, and opioid agents slow motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); First Aid Gastrointestinal (anticonstipation drugs). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactulose also treats hepatic encephalopathy because gut bacteria acidify the colon and trap NH4+ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: bacteria degrade some nonabsorbed solutes into more osmotically active particles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major cause of secretory diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border lactase deficiency","B":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","C":"Nonabsorbable laxative ingestion","D":"Loss of absorptive mucosal area","E":"Enteropathic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo names overgrowth of enteropathic bacteria such as Vibrio cholerae or Escherichia coli as the major cause of secretory diarrhea. Lactase deficiency and laxatives cause osmotic diarrhea, pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption, and reduced mucosal area is a separate category. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: enteritis increases mucosal secretion and motility to flush the infectious agent (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Diarrheal fluid is rich in which anion because intestinal secretions contain it?","choices":{"A":"Chloride ion","B":"Phosphate ion","C":"Bicarbonate","D":"Sulfate ion","E":"Lactate ion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salivary, pancreatic, and intestinal juices have a high HCO3- content, so diarrheal fluid does too. Loss of HCO3- relative to Cl- causes hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate. Phosphate, sulfate, and lactate are not the named anion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal fluid is also high in K+ from flow-dependent colonic secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has a high-output jejunostomy after bowel surgery. He drinks about 2 L per day, yet stoma output is 3.5 L per day. Blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg, and BUN is 31 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL). Which best explains why output can exceed what he drinks?","choices":{"A":"Upper gut secretions add to dietary fluid","B":"Excess colonic secretion of potassium-rich fluid","C":"Renal water loss into the bowel","D":"Bacterial fermentation of dietary starch","E":"Stoma mucosa secreting mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"About 7 L of salivary, gastric, pancreatic, and biliary secretions joins dietary fluid each day. Normally the distal small intestine and colon reabsorb it, so losing them lets secreted fluid pour out. The colon is bypassed, the kidney does not empty into the bowel, and fermentation and mucus are minor. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a high-output stoma can cause dehydration and prerenal azotemia because proximal secretions are not reabsorbed (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion adds to luminal fluid","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man receives a hypertonic enteral feed directly into the jejunum and develops cramps and watery output. Temperature is 37.0°C, and serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which process best explains the early increase in luminal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Active water pumping by crypt epithelial cells","B":"Closure of jejunal tight junctions","C":"Water entry until contents are isosmotic","D":"Toxin-driven chloride channel opening","E":"Aldosterone-driven colonic secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Water crosses the leaky jejunal epithelium osmotically, so hypertonic contents draw water into the lumen until they approach isosmolarity. Water does not move by active pumping. Jejunal tight junctions are leaky. No toxin is present, and aldosterone acts on colonic absorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperosmotic luminal contents draw water into the lumen until they become isosmotic (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intestinal absorbate is isosmotic, much like proximal tubular fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — duodenal osmolality and gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has an end ileostomy after colectomy. Her stoma effluent sodium stays close to her plasma sodium, whereas normal stool sodium is much lower. Blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Which feature of the small intestine best explains why it fails to lower effluent sodium as the colon would?","choices":{"A":"Absent basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","B":"Resistance of enterocytes to aldosterone","C":"High colonic potassium secretion","D":"Bile salt-driven crypt secretion","E":"Leaky low-resistance tight junctions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Small-intestinal tight junctions are low resistance and permit paracellular movement, so Na+ leaks back and a steep gradient against the lumen is hard to hold. The colon has tight high-resistance junctions and aldosterone-induced Na+ channels. The Na+-K+ ATPase is present, and colonic K+ secretion is absent after colectomy. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). The effluent-sodium comparison is inferred from tight-junction resistance. Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aldosterone increases colonic Na+ absorption through apical Na+ channels (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: loss of the colon removes the high-resistance segment that finishes salt and water absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman has 3 days of profuse watery diarrhea from norovirus. She is weak, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which mechanism most directly explains her low potassium?","choices":{"A":"Renal K⁺ wasting from loop diuretics","B":"Shift of K⁺ into cells from alkalosis","C":"Reduced dietary potassium intake","D":"Flow-dependent colonic K⁺ secretion","E":"Crypt-cell K⁺ secretion via channels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As in renal principal cells, colonic K+ secretion depends on flow. High diarrheal flow increases K+ secretion and fecal K+ loss, causing hypokalemia. No diuretic is given, diarrhea causes acidosis rather than alkalosis, intake is not the main factor, and crypt cells secrete Cl-. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypokalemia can cause muscle weakness and flattened T waves (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: aldosterone also increases colonic K+ secretion through Na+ channel synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man has hypertension and muscle cramps. Serum potassium is 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), plasma renin is suppressed, and an adrenal adenoma is found. Which change in his colonic epithelium is expected?","choices":{"A":"Reduced colonic K⁺ secretion","B":"Increased opening of crypt Cl⁻ channels by cAMP","C":"Increased Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchange activity","D":"Decreased basolateral K⁺ uptake","E":"Increased apical Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aldosterone induces synthesis of apical Na+ channels in colonocytes. Na+ absorption rises, and K+ secretion rises secondarily through increased pumping, adding to K+ loss. Decreased channel synthesis is the opposite. Crypt Cl- channels respond to cAMP agonists, and Cl--HCO3- exchange is ileal. Basolateral K+ uptake increases. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism causes hypertension, hypokalemia, and low renin (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: the colonic transport pattern mirrors the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — colonic electrolyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman takes a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor for glaucoma. Serum bicarbonate is 19 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which jejunal transport step is most directly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Apical Na⁺-glucose cotransport by SGLT1 carriers","B":"Supply of H⁺ for apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","C":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Apical Na⁺ channel insertion by aldosterone","E":"Paracellular water absorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the jejunum, carbonic anhydrase converts CO2 and H2O to H+ and HCO3-. H+ is secreted on the Na+-H+ exchanger and HCO3- is absorbed. Inhibiting the enzyme limits H+ supply for that exchange and HCO3- absorption. Na+-glucose cotransport is independent, the three-ion cotransporter is in crypt cells, and apical Na+ channels are colonic. Paracellular water absorption follows solute osmotically and does not depend on carbonic anhydrase generating H⁺. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). The drug effect on gut is inferred from the mechanism. Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause metabolic acidosis by reducing proximal tubular HCO3- reabsorption (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: jejunal electrolyte transport is identical to that of the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — carbonic anhydrase and H⁺/HCO₃⁻ generation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Paracellular water absorption follows solute osmotically and"}},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has abdominal distension and fails to pass meconium. Contrast enema shows a microcolon with inspissated meconium in the distal ileum. Sweat chloride later returns 78 mmol/L (reference range: <30 mmol/L). Which defect best explains the thick intestinal contents?","choices":{"A":"Impaired apical Cl⁻ secretion by crypt cells","B":"Excess Na⁺ absorption by colonic channels","C":"Loss of basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Absent Na⁺-glucose cotransport on villus enterocytes","E":"Overactive apical anion exchange"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Crypt Cl- secretion exits through apical Cl- channels, the CFTR channel. Without it, Cl-, Na+, and water are not secreted, and luminal contents become dehydrated. The basolateral cotransporter is intact, and absorption by villus Na+-glucose cotransport is not the defect. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: Na+ follows secreted Cl- passively between cells, and water follows NaCl (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has 3 L per day of watery diarrhea that continues while she is fasting. Serum potassium is 2.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and gastric acid output is low. CT shows a pancreatic tail mass. Which mechanism best explains her diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Nonabsorbed solute holding water","B":"Bile acid stimulation of colonic epithelium","C":"Peptide-driven crypt-cell cAMP excess","D":"Mucosal invasion by bacteria","E":"Rapid transit from gastrin excess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A VIP-secreting tumor activates basolateral receptors on crypt cells, raising cAMP and opening apical Cl- channels. Na+ and water follow, causing large-volume secretory diarrhea that continues during fasting. Nonabsorbed solute would stop with fasting, no ileal disease is present, no invasion is suggested, and gastrin is not the hormone. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37); First Aid Gastrointestinal (VIP). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (WDHA syndrome) (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: octreotide is used to treat VIPoma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — VIPoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old farmworker has vomiting, drooling, small pupils, and profuse watery diarrhea after spraying an organophosphate pesticide. Heart rate is 48/min. Plasma cholinesterase is 900 U/L (reference range: 3000–8000 U/L). Which mechanism best explains increased intestinal fluid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Excess VIP release from a pancreatic islet tumor","B":"Excess acetylcholine at crypt cell receptors","C":"Toxin-mediated Gs activation in crypt cells","D":"Nonabsorbed pesticide holding water","E":"Loss of colonic Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo lists acetylcholine among the basolateral agonists that open apical Cl- channels in crypt cells. Cholinesterase inhibition lets acetylcholine build up, so Cl-, Na+, and water secretion increases. VIP excess is a tumor syndrome, ADP-ribosylation is the toxin mechanism, the pesticide is not an osmotic load, and aldosterone controls colonic channels. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: organophosphates irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase and cause a cholinergic toxidrome (First Aid Pharmacology). COMLEX: atropine blocks muscarinic effects and pralidoxime regenerates acetylcholinesterase (First Aid Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system and autonomic input","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker has profuse rice-water stools 2 days after drinking untreated water. Blood pressure is 92/58 mm Hg, and stool culture grows Vibrio cholerae. Which intracellular messenger remains persistently high in his crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Inositol trisphosphate","D":"Diacylglycerol","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Toxin-modified αs keeps adenylyl cyclase active, so cAMP stays high and apical Cl- channels stay open. Cyclic GMP is the messenger of heat-stable E. coli toxin, not cholera. IP3 and diacylglycerol come from Gq signaling, and nitric oxide relaxes smooth muscle. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin causes secretory diarrhea by permanently activating Gs (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intravenous replacement of fluid and electrolytes is the main basis of therapy in severe cholera (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Gs/cAMP receptor signaling pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman returns from Mexico with 4 days of watery diarrhea from toxigenic Escherichia coli. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L), chloride 114 mEq/L (reference range: 98–106 mEq/L), and bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is most likely?","choices":{"A":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis","B":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis","C":"Acute respiratory acidosis","D":"Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis","E":"Acute respiratory alkalosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretions lost in diarrhea are rich in HCO3-, so HCO3- falls relative to Cl-. The anion gap is 138 − (114 + 15) = 9, normal, which fits hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. A high gap would need unmeasured acids. Alkalosis follows vomiting, and respiratory disorders are not primary. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea is a classic cause of normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Escherichia coli enterotoxins cause secretory diarrhea like cholera toxin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy in a flood-relief camp has had 12 watery stools in a day. He is lethargic with sunken eyes. Heart rate is 168/min, and blood pressure is 70/40 mm Hg. Which consequence of his secretory diarrhea most directly threatens his life?","choices":{"A":"Hyperkalemic cardiac arrhythmia","B":"Loss of extracellular fluid volume","C":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis from vomiting","D":"Bacteremic septic shock","E":"Hypernatremic seizures"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diarrhea loses extracellular-type fluid, decreasing ECF and intravascular volume and arterial pressure. Baroreceptor and renin-angiotensin-aldosterone responses fail if the loss is too great or too fast, leading to circulatory collapse. K+ is lost rather than gained, the acidosis is not alkalosis, and toxin-mediated diarrhea is not invasive. Hypernatremic seizures do not fit, because diarrheal secretions are isosmotic extracellular-type fluid whose loss shrinks volume rather than raising sodium. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without fluid replacement, up to 50 percent of patients with cholera die (Guyton Ch. 67). COMLEX: antibiotics can shorten the duration and volume of cholera diarrhea (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Hypernatremic seizures do not fit, because diarrheal secreti"}},{"id":"sdl28_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man starts milk of magnesia daily for constipation and develops watery diarrhea. It stops within a day when he is kept nil by mouth for a procedure. Serum magnesium is 2.6 mg/dL (reference range: 1.7–2.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Peptide-driven crypt Cl⁻ secretion","B":"Toxin-driven Gs activation in crypt cells","C":"Inflammatory loss of villus absorptive surface","D":"Unabsorbed cations retaining luminal water","E":"Bile acid stimulation of colonocytes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Poorly absorbed magnesium stays in the lumen and holds water, an osmotic diarrhea that stops when the solute is stopped. Peptide-driven or toxin-driven crypt secretion persists during fasting. Villous injury and bile acid diarrhea do not fit a laxative that stopped. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); First Aid Gastrointestinal (anticonstipation drugs). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic laxatives such as magnesium salts may be misused by patients with bulimia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: secretory diarrhea results from excess crypt-cell secretion rather than inadequate absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — lactase deficiency as osmotic diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old girl has 4 days of vomiting and watery diarrhea during a rotavirus outbreak. Biopsy in a similar case shows blunted villi. Heart rate is 150/min, and capillary refill is 3 seconds. Which category of diarrhea best describes her primary mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Toxin-driven crypt secretion","B":"Peptide hormone-driven secretion","C":"Nonabsorbable osmotic laxative load","D":"Aldosterone-driven colonic K⁺ loss","E":"Decreased absorptive surface area"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo classifies diarrhea into decreased absorptive surface area, osmotic, and secretory types. Infection and inflammation of the small intestine reduce the surface that absorbs fluid. Toxin-driven and hormone-driven crypt secretion are secretory types, and laxatives are osmotic. Aldosterone does not cause diarrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rotavirus is a leading cause of watery diarrhea in young children and is preventable by vaccine (First Aid Microbiology). COMLEX: viral injury to villus tips can cause secondary lactase deficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl28_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had 5 days of profuse watery diarrhea. She feels dizzy on standing. Blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg, and urine sodium is 8 mEq/L (reference range: 40–220 mEq/L). Which compensatory response is most likely active?","choices":{"A":"Suppressed antidiuretic hormone release","B":"Increased atrial natriuretic peptide","C":"Activated renin-angiotensin-aldosterone","D":"Reduced sympathetic outflow","E":"Suppressed baroreceptor reflex activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure. Baroreceptors increase sympathetic outflow, and the renin-angiotensin II-aldosterone system is activated to retain Na+, giving low urine sodium. ADH increases rather than falls, ANP falls with low volume, and sympathetic and baroreceptor responses increase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low urine sodium in hypovolemia reflects avid renal sodium retention (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: compensation fails if diarrheal fluid loss is too large or too rapid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with an ileostomy asks which bowel segment normally has the tightest epithelial junctions. Which segment has high-resistance tight junctions?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Jejunum","C":"Colon","D":"Ileum","E":"Stomach"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Colonic tight junctions are tight, with high resistance, and do not permit paracellular movement. Small-intestinal tight junctions are leaky and permit substantial paracellular flow. The stomach is not described in this comparison. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic transport resembles that of renal principal cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: tight-junction permeability decides whether fluid moves by cellular or paracellular routes (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has extensive Crohn disease of the small bowel. Which small-intestinal segment is normally the major site of Na⁺ absorption?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Ileum","C":"Cecum","D":"Rectum","E":"Jejunum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo names the jejunum as the major site of Na+ absorption in the small intestine, through Na+-coupled cotransporters and Na+-H+ exchange. The ileum adds Cl--HCO3- exchange. The cecum and rectum are colon. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease can involve any part of the GI tract, most often the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: Na+ entering jejunal cells is pumped out by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis has recurrent distal intestinal obstruction. Which protein is defective in his intestinal crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"CFTR chloride channel","B":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","C":"Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","D":"Epithelial Na⁺ channel","E":"H⁺-K⁺ ATPase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CFTR is the apical Cl- channel through which crypt cells secrete Cl-, with Na+ and water following. The basolateral three-ion cotransporter, Na+-H+ exchange, and epithelial Na+ channels are intact, and H+-K+ ATPase is gastric. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without crypt Cl- secretion, Na+ and water do not follow into the lumen, so contents become dehydrated (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cAMP opens apical Cl- channels in crypt cells (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has severe rice-water diarrhea from Vibrio cholerae. Which enzyme stays persistently active in his crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"Guanylyl cyclase","B":"Phospholipase C","C":"Protein kinase C","D":"Adenylyl cyclase","E":"Carbonic anhydrase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates αs, so Gs keeps adenylyl cyclase active and cAMP stays high. Guanylyl cyclase is targeted by heat-stable E. coli toxin, phospholipase C and protein kinase C belong to Gq signaling, and carbonic anhydrase supplies H+ and HCO3-. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Secretory diarrhea, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates αs, keeping Gs active and cAMP high (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cholera can secrete 10 to 12 L per day into the intestine (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor causing large-volume watery diarrhea despite fasting. Which drug reduces the tumor's hormone secretion?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Octreotide","C":"Lactulose","D":"Bisacodyl","E":"Psyllium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Octreotide is a long-acting somatostatin analog used for VIPoma that inhibits secretion of splanchnic vasodilatory hormones. Loperamide slows motility, lactulose is an osmotic laxative, bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative, and psyllium is bulk-forming. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (octreotide); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is also used for acute variceal bleeds, acromegaly, and carcinoid tumors (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide). COMLEX: octreotide can cause steatorrhea and cholelithiasis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 22-year-old woman eats a normal diet. About how much water does she lose in feces each day?","choices":{"A":"About 100–200 mL","B":"About 1–2 L","C":"About 2–3 L","D":"About 500–800 mL","E":"About 5–10 mL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Of slightly more than 9 L that enters the lumen daily, almost all is absorbed by the small intestine and colon, leaving about 100–200 mL in feces. Losses of liters per day mean diarrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport, Fig. 8.34). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea can lose 9 L or more of fluid per day (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: about 2 L of daily luminal fluid comes from the diet (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"grid_sdl28_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":4,"stem":"A 30-year-old man in a cholera outbreak has had 2 days of profuse rice-water stools. Blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg, and heart rate is 124/min. Stool culture grows Vibrio cholerae. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely before treatment?","grid":{"headers":["Plasma aldosterone","Serum K⁺","Serum HCO₃⁻"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↓","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↓"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↑","↔","↑"],"E":["↓","↑","↑"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↓ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) is correct. Loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure and activates the renin-angiotensin II-aldosterone system. Diarrheal fluid is high in K⁺ from flow-dependent colonic secretion, which aldosterone adds to, so hypokalemia follows. Diarrheal fluid is also rich in HCO₃⁻ from intestinal secretions, causing hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Choice A (↓ plasma aldosterone, ↓ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) misses the volume response. Choice B (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↑ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) ignores fecal K⁺ loss. Choice D (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↔ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻) fits vomiting more than diarrhea, with loss of gastric H⁺ raising HCO₃⁻. Choice E (↓ plasma aldosterone, ↑ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻) reverses each change. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Colon; Diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, whereas vomiting causes metabolic alkalosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement is the main basis of therapy in severe cholera (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true}],"29":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of mechanical intestinal obstructions are caused by the four major mechanical causes combined?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent","B":"About 30 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 95 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus together cause about 80% of mechanical obstructions. Tumors, infarction, and strictures such as Crohn disease make up the rest. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 29 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: small bowel obstruction is most commonly caused by intraperitoneal adhesions, tumors, and hernias (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction). COMLEX: upright abdominal x-ray in small bowel obstruction shows air-fluid levels (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide?","choices":{"A":"Adhesions","B":"Volvulus","C":"Tumors","D":"Hernias","E":"Strictures"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hernias are the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide and the third most common in the United States. Inguinal hernias, with narrow orifices and large sacs, are most often involved. Adhesions are the leading cause of obstruction in the United States and developed nations, not worldwide. Volvulus is rare and occurs most often in the sigmoid colon, so it is not the most frequent cause of obstruction. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hernias). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: femoral hernias protrude below the inguinal ligament and carry a greater risk of strangulation than inguinal hernias (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hernias). COMLEX: acquired hernias are found in up to 5% of the population (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Adhesions are the leading cause of obstruction in the United","B":"Volvulus is rare and occurs most often in the sigmoid colon,"}},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the segment of bowel that telescopes into the distal bowel in intussusception called?","choices":{"A":"Intussusceptum","B":"Intussuscipiens","C":"Hernia sac","D":"Mesenteric root","E":"Closed loop"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In intussusception, a segment constricted by a peristaltic wave (the intussusceptum) telescopes into the immediately distal segment (the intussuscipiens). The hernia sac is the serosa-lined peritoneal pouch that protrudes through an abdominal wall defect, not a telescoping bowel segment. A closed loop is formed by fibrous adhesions that trap sliding viscera, an internal hernia mechanism rather than telescoping. The mesentery is pulled along behind the telescoping intussusceptum; it is not the segment that telescopes. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intussusception most commonly occurs at the ileocecal junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception). COMLEX: ultrasound or CT of intussusception may show a target sign (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The hernia sac is the serosa-lined peritoneal pouch that pro","E":"A closed loop is formed by fibrous adhesions that trap slidi","D":"The mesentery is pulled along behind the telescoping intussu"}},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"What causes most acute arterial obstruction of the mesenteric circulation?","choices":{"A":"Systemic vasculitis","B":"Mesenteric venous clot","C":"Tumor compression","D":"Thrombosis or embolism","E":"Vasoconstrictive drugs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In a large majority of cases, acute mesenteric obstruction is caused by thrombosis or embolism. Severe atherosclerosis is the main risk for thrombosis, and emboli come mostly from aortic atheromas or cardiac mural thrombi. Mesenteric venous thrombosis is a venous cause tied to hypercoagulable states, cirrhosis, and neoplasms, not acute arterial obstruction. Vasoconstrictive drugs such as cocaine cause nonocclusive hypoperfusion rather than arterial obstruction. Vasculitides are a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thrombosis than atherosclerotic thrombosis or embolism. Invasive neoplasms are a cause of mesenteric venous thrombosis, whereas acute arterial obstruction is mostly thrombosis or embolism. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for acute mesenteric ischemia include atrial fibrillation, peripheral arterial disease, recent MI, and CHF (First Aid Gastrointestinal, acute mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: mesenteric venous thrombosis is an uncommon cause of ischemic bowel disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis is a venous cause tied to hyper","E":"Vasoconstrictive drugs such as cocaine cause nonocclusive hy","A":"Vasculitides are a less common cause of mesenteric arterial ","C":"Invasive neoplasms are a cause of mesenteric venous thrombos"}},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal component is most vulnerable to ischemic injury?","choices":{"A":"Surface epithelium","B":"Crypt stem cells","C":"Basal crypt cells","D":"Muscularis propria","E":"Myenteric neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Capillaries run from crypt to surface before a hairpin turn, so the surface epithelium is most vulnerable while crypt stem cells are protected. Surface atrophy with normal or hyperproliferative crypts is the signature of ischemia. The muscularis propria is injured only in transmural infarction, typically from acute major vascular occlusion. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia causes postprandial epigastric pain, food aversion, and weight loss (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: chronic intestinal ischemia is accompanied by fibrous scarring of the lamina propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The muscularis propria is injured only in transmural infarct"}},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"In which age group is ischemic disease of the colon most common?","choices":{"A":"Younger than 2 years","B":"Between 20 and 30","C":"Older than 70 years","D":"Between 30 and 40","E":"Between 40 and 50"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Colonic ischemia is most common in patients older than 70 years and is slightly more common in females. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia shows a thumbprint sign on imaging from mucosal edema and hemorrhage (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: mortality in the first 30 days of colonic ischemia is about 10% with appropriate management (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is angiodysplasia most common?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum region","B":"Cecum or right colon","C":"Proximal jejunal loops","D":"Sigmoid colon segment","E":"Lower rectum and anus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon and usually comes to attention after the sixth decade. The narrower sigmoid colon lacks the cecum's large diameter and high wall tension that drive angiodysplasia. Angiodysplasia arises from distention-related occlusion of submucosal colonic veins and is most common in the cecum or right colon, not the stomach. Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon, where diameter and wall tension are greatest, not in the proximal jejunum. Angiodysplasia favors the cecum or right colon, the segment with the largest diameter and greatest wall tension, not the rectum and anus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia presents with hematochezia from tortuous dilated vessels (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia bleeding ranges from chronic and intermittent to acute and massive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The narrower sigmoid colon lacks the cecum's large diameter ","A":"Angiodysplasia arises from distention-related occlusion of s","C":"Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon, w","E":"Angiodysplasia favors the cecum or right colon, the segment "}},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which test confirms angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Barium swallow","B":"Stool culture","C":"Plain radiograph","D":"Angiography","E":"Breath testing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Visceral or aortic angiography (with colonoscopy) is the diagnostic test for angiodysplasia. The lesion consists of malformed, dilated, thin-walled submucosal and mucosal vessels. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is more common in older patients (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia has a prevalence of less than 1% in adults (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of major lower intestinal bleeding episodes in older people is due to angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"About 1 percent","B":"About 5 percent","C":"About 20 percent","D":"About 50 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Although its prevalence is less than 1% in adults, angiodysplasia causes about 20% of major lower intestinal bleeding episodes in older populations. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lower GI bleeding causes include diverticulosis, angiodysplasia, and hemorrhoids (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI bleeding). COMLEX: angiodysplastic channels are separated from the lumen only by the thin vessel wall and attenuated epithelium (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What share of patients with cystic fibrosis eventually develop exocrine pancreatic insufficiency?","choices":{"A":"Under 1 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 50 percent","E":"Over 80 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In cystic fibrosis, intraductal pancreatic concretions cause duct obstruction, chronic pancreatitis, and exocrine pancreatic insufficiency in more than 80% of patients. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Cystic fibrosis). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat and vitamins A, D, E, and K as well as vitamin B12 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic insufficiency). COMLEX: CF pancreatic concretions can begin forming in utero (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"IgG antigliadin antibodies","B":"IgA anti-TG2 antibodies","C":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies","D":"Antimitochondrial antibodies","E":"Perinuclear ANCA antibodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most sensitive noninvasive test for celiac disease is IgA against tissue transglutaminase 2. IgG anti-TG2 can be used in patients with IgA deficiency. IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase (TG2) are the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease, ahead of antigliadin antibodies. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac serology also includes anti-endomysial and anti-deamidated gliadin peptide antibodies (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease shows an abnormal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase (TG2) are the most"}},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common cancer in people with celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"Gastric diffuse large B-cell lymphoma tumor","B":"Distal esophageal Barrett adenocarcinoma","C":"Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma of the liver","E":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The most common cancer in celiac disease is enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma, an aggressive lymphoma of intraepithelial T cells. Small intestinal adenocarcinoma is also more frequent. The most common cancer in celiac disease is a T-cell lymphoma of the intestine (EATL), not a gastric B-cell lymphoma. The cancers increased in celiac disease are enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (most common) and small intestinal adenocarcinoma, not Barrett adenocarcinoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with dermatitis herpetiformis and decreased bone density (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: a gluten-free diet may reduce celiac complications including anemia, female infertility, and osteoporosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The most common cancer in celiac disease is a T-cell lymphom","B":"The cancers increased in celiac disease are enteropathy-asso"}},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Mutation of which gene causes IPEX?","choices":{"A":"CFTR","B":"MYO5B","C":"HLA-DQ2","D":"RET","E":"FOXP3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IPEX (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linkage) is caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked FOXP3 gene. CFTR mutation causes cystic fibrosis, impairing chloride and bicarbonate transport and leading to pancreatic insufficiency. HLA-DQ2 is the susceptibility allele of celiac disease, present in about 90% of cases. MYO5B mutations cause microvillus inclusion disease, not IPEX. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microvillus inclusion disease is caused by MYO5B mutations and presents with intractable diarrhea in early infancy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: autoimmune enteropathy is treated with immunosuppressants such as cyclosporine or with stem cell transplantation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR mutation causes cystic fibrosis, impairing chloride and","C":"HLA-DQ2 is the susceptibility allele of celiac disease, pres","B":"MYO5B mutations cause microvillus inclusion disease, not IPE"}},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which defective molecule causes abetalipoproteinemia?","choices":{"A":"Cystic fibrosis transmembrane regulator","B":"Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein","C":"Sodium-glucose linked transporter protein","D":"Low-density lipoprotein receptor protein","E":"Apolipoprotein E lipid carrier protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Abetalipoproteinemia is an autosomal recessive defect in microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP), which transfers lipids to nascent apolipoprotein B in the ER. CFTR dysfunction causes cystic fibrosis, whose malabsorption comes from pancreatic insufficiency rather than failed lipoprotein assembly. Abetalipoproteinemia results from loss of microsomal triglyceride transfer protein, which loads lipids onto apoB in the endoplasmic reticulum; the LDL receptor is not the defect. The defect in abetalipoproteinemia is microsomal triglyceride transfer protein, so apoB-containing lipoproteins are absent; apolipoprotein E is not the missing molecule. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia causes acanthocytes on the peripheral smear (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia presents in infancy with failure to thrive, diarrhea, and steatorrhea (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR dysfunction causes cystic fibrosis, whose malabsorption","D":"Abetalipoproteinemia results from loss of microsomal triglyc","E":"The defect in abetalipoproteinemia is microsomal triglycerid"}},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"About how many children worldwide are estimated to be affected by the enteropathy linked to poor sanitation and chronic fecal pathogen exposure?","choices":{"A":"Over 150 million","B":"Around 1 million","C":"Around 10 million","D":"Around 25 million","E":"Around 50 million"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Environmental enteric dysfunction is estimated to affect more than 150 million children worldwide and may contribute to many childhood deaths. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tropical sprue responds to antibiotics and is associated with folate and later B12 deficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, tropical sprue). COMLEX: high oral vaccine failure rates where EED is endemic suggest a mucosal immune defect (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-month-old boy has intermittent screaming episodes with his legs drawn up and red currant-jelly stools after a recent viral illness. A sausage-shaped right-sided mass is felt. What most likely served as the leading edge?","choices":{"A":"Intraluminal adenocarcinoma","B":"Inspissated meconium plug","C":"Fibrous adhesion band","D":"Hyperplastic Peyer patches","E":"Incarcerated hernia sac"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intussusception is the most common cause of obstruction under age 2. Idiopathic cases follow viral infection or rotavirus vaccine, probably because reactive Peyer patch hyperplasia forms the leading edge. An intraluminal tumor is the usual traction point in adults and older children, not in an infant after a viral illness. Inspissated stool acts as the traction point in about 1% of patients with cystic fibrosis, which this child lacks. Fibrous adhesion bands follow prior abdominal surgery or infection and are an adult cause of obstruction. An incarcerated hernia traps bowel in a sac through a wall defect and does not create a telescoping lead point. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25); First Aid Gastrointestinal (intussusception). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in idiopathic intussusception of young children, contrast or air enemas are both diagnostic and therapeutic (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum and bowel wall hematoma from IgA vasculitis are pediatric intussusception lead points (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An intraluminal tumor is the usual traction point in adults ","B":"Inspissated stool acts as the traction point in about 1% of ","C":"Fibrous adhesion bands follow prior abdominal surgery or inf","E":"An incarcerated hernia traps bowel in a sac through a wall d"}},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman who had a hysterectomy years ago has abdominal pain, distention, and vomiting. She has no external hernia, and x-ray shows air-fluid levels. What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Twisting of the sigmoid colon","B":"Postoperative fibrous bands","C":"Ileocecal segment telescoping","D":"Inguinal sac herniation of bowel","E":"Gallstone lodged at the valve"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surgery, infection, or endometriosis can produce fibrous adhesions that create closed loops. Other viscera slide through and become trapped as an internal hernia, with obstruction and strangulation. Sigmoid volvulus twists a redundant sigmoid loop, whereas prior hysterectomy points to adhesions forming closed loops. Ileocecal telescoping in adults usually needs a tumor as traction point, and prior pelvic surgery instead points to adhesions. Inguinal herniation is unlikely with no external hernia; adhesion bands instead cause internal herniation. After surgery, fibrous adhesions can form bridges that create closed loops and internal herniation, which fits obstruction years after a hysterectomy better than a gallstone. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adhesions); First Aid Gastrointestinal (small bowel obstruction). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: small bowel obstruction is managed with GI decompression, volume resuscitation, and bowel rest (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction). COMLEX: adhesions can follow peritoneal inflammation such as endometriosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sigmoid volvulus twists a redundant sigmoid loop, whereas pr","C":"Ileocecal telescoping in adults usually needs a tumor as tra","D":"Inguinal herniation is unlikely with no external hernia; adh","E":"After surgery, fibrous adhesions can form bridges that creat"}},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man in a nursing home with chronic constipation has sudden abdominal distention. Abdominal x-ray shows a coffee-bean sign. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Midgut volvulus","B":"Cecal carcinoma","C":"Sigmoid volvulus","D":"Colonic ileus","E":"Ischemic colitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Volvulus most often involves large redundant sigmoid loops, followed by the cecum. Sigmoid volvulus is more common in older adults and shows a coffee-bean sign. Midgut volvulus affects infants with malrotation, not an older adult with chronic constipation. Ischemic colitis presents with cramping left lower quadrant pain and bloody diarrhea, not a twisted loop on x-ray. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Volvulus); First Aid Gastrointestinal (volvulus). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric volvulus in adults is more common with paraesophageal hernia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus). COMLEX: because it is rare, volvulus can be overlooked clinically with catastrophic results (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Midgut volvulus affects infants with malrotation, not an old","E":"Ischemic colitis presents with cramping left lower quadrant "}},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe abdominal pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen on exam. What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Embolic occlusion of the SMA","B":"Thrombosis of a mesenteric vein","C":"Twisting of the sigmoid mesentery","D":"Vasculitis of small serosal arteries","E":"Rupture of a cecal vascular ectasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acute mesenteric ischemia is a critical blockage of intestinal flow, often embolic SMA occlusion, with pain out of proportion to exam. Emboli commonly come from cardiac mural thrombi. Mesenteric venous thrombosis arises with hypercoagulable states, cirrhosis, or neoplasms, while atrial fibrillation favors arterial emboli. Sigmoid volvulus is a twisting obstruction of redundant sigmoid loops, not an embolic event linked to atrial fibrillation. Cecal vascular ectasia (angiodysplasia) presents with lower GI bleeding in older adults rather than sudden severe pain. Vasculitis is a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thrombosis, whereas atrial fibrillation supplies emboli, one of the two main causes of acute arterial obstruction. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease); First Aid Gastrointestinal (acute mesenteric ischemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute arterial infarction produces a sharp demarcation between normal and ischemic bowel (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: coagulative necrosis of the muscularis propria develops within 1 to 4 days of infarction (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis arises with hypercoagulable sta","C":"Sigmoid volvulus is a twisting obstruction of redundant sigm","E":"Cecal vascular ectasia (angiodysplasia) presents with lower ","D":"Vasculitis is a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thr"}},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman recovering from septic shock has crampy left lower abdominal pain, then bloody diarrhea. A colonic biopsy is taken. Which finding best supports ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas scattered in lamina propria","B":"Crypt abscesses in diffuse colitis","C":"Villous blunting with lymphocytes","D":"Upper mucosa lost but crypts spared","E":"Thickened subepithelial collagen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ischemic injury progresses from the lumen downward because crypts receive blood first. Atrophy or sloughing of the upper mucosa with normal or hyperproliferative crypts is the morphologic signature. Villous blunting with intraepithelial lymphocytes is the celiac pattern, not the ischemic pattern of surface loss with spared crypts. A thickened subepithelial collagen band (more than 10 µm) defines collagenous colitis, which presents with watery, not bloody, diarrhea. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia commonly occurs at the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: neutrophils are recruited into ischemic mucosa within hours of reperfusion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Villous blunting with intraepithelial lymphocytes is the cel","E":"A thickened subepithelial collagen band (more than 10 µm) de"}},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has ischemic colitis confined to the right colon. Why does this location carry a worse prognosis?","choices":{"A":"The right colon wall is especially thin","B":"The right colon lacks a submucosal layer","C":"The right colon has no collateral arcades","D":"Bacteria are far denser in the right colon","E":"Its artery also feeds the small intestine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mortality is doubled in right-sided colonic ischemia. The right colon is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery, which also supplies much of the small intestine, so right-sided disease may signal wider compromise. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia most commonly involves the SMA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: COPD and symptoms lasting more than 2 weeks are poor prognostic signs in colonic ischemia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with aortic stenosis has recurrent painless hematochezia and iron deficiency. Colonoscopy finds small flat red mucosal lesions. What will histology most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Glands showing high-grade dysplasia","B":"Granulation tissue in an ulcer bed","C":"Ectatic nests of thin-walled vessels","D":"Mucosal outpouching through the muscle","E":"Noncaseating granulomas in the mucosa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Angiodysplasia consists of ectatic nests of tortuous veins, venules, and capillaries, separated from the lumen only by a thin wall and attenuated epithelium. Minor injury makes them bleed. Mucosal outpouching through the muscle wall describes a diverticulum, whereas angiodysplasia shows ectatic nests of tortuous veins, venules, and capillaries. Angiodysplasia histology shows ectatic nests of tortuous thin-walled vessels; granulomas are not a feature. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia); First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: false diverticula occur where vasa recta penetrate the muscularis externa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, diverticula of the GI tract). COMLEX: angiodysplasia usually comes to clinical attention after the sixth decade (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Mucosal outpouching through the muscle wall describes a dive","E":"Angiodysplasia histology shows ectatic nests of tortuous thi"}},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has bleeding from colonic angiodysplasia. Which mechanical factor is thought to explain its typical location?","choices":{"A":"Lowest blood flow at a watershed zone","B":"Thinnest muscularis of any colonic part","C":"Highest bacterial count in the colon lumen","D":"Largest diameter gives most wall tension","E":"Most lymphoid tissue in the large bowel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because the cecum has the largest diameter of any colonic segment, it develops the greatest wall tension. This may explain the cecal and right-colon predominance of angiodysplasia. Watershed zones are the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid, which explain ischemic colitis sites rather than cecal angiodysplasia. The cecal location of angiodysplasia is explained by its having the largest diameter and so the greatest wall tension, not by a thinner muscularis. The proposed mechanism for angiodysplasia is mechanical: distention and contraction occlude submucosal veins, worst in the wide cecum. Bacterial load plays no part. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia in older patients is associated with aortic stenosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: the pathogenesis of angiodysplasia has been attributed to mechanical and congenital factors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Watershed zones are the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid, wh","B":"The cecal location of angiodysplasia is explained by its hav","C":"The proposed mechanism for angiodysplasia is mechanical: dis"}},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has recurrent hematochezia from colonic angiodysplasia. Which of his conditions is classically associated with this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus","B":"End-stage renal disease","C":"Primary hypothyroidism","D":"Gouty arthritis history","E":"Chronic obstructive lung"},"correct":"B","explanation":"First Aid associates angiodysplasia with end-stage renal disease, von Willebrand disease, and aortic stenosis. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is confirmed by angiography (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: normal gut distention may intermittently occlude submucosal veins crossing the muscularis propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has bloating, iron deficiency anemia, and an itchy blistering rash on her elbows. A biopsy of the second part of the duodenum is taken. What will it most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lymphocytosis with villous atrophy","B":"Vacuolated enterocytes filled with lipid","C":"Foamy macrophages packed in the lamina","D":"Transmural granulomas with deep fissuring","E":"Normal villi without inflammatory cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Celiac disease shows increased intraepithelial CD8+ T cells, crypt hyperplasia, and villous atrophy, best seen in the second part of the duodenum or proximal jejunum. Dermatitis herpetiformis occurs in up to 10% of patients. Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes are the abetalipoproteinemia pattern, from triglyceride that MTP defects leave trapped in cells. Normal villi fit lactase deficiency, a purely biochemical defect, not celiac disease with dermatitis herpetiformis. Lamina propria packed with PAS-positive foamy macrophages is the picture of Whipple disease, not celiac disease with dermatitis herpetiformis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease); First Aid Gastrointestinal (celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease in adults most commonly presents between ages 30 and 60 (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes are the abetalipoproteinemia pa","E":"Normal villi fit lactase deficiency, a purely biochemical de","C":"Lamina propria packed with PAS-positive foamy macrophages is"}},{"id":"sdl29_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man with chronic diarrhea has suspected gluten-sensitive enteropathy, and HLA-DQ typing is ordered. How is this genetic result most useful?","choices":{"A":"Positive result confirms the diagnosis","B":"Result predicts the lymphoma risk","C":"Result sets the diet duration needed","D":"Result replaces the need for biopsy","E":"Negative result argues against it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HLA-DQ2 is present in about 90% of patients, with DQ8 in most of the rest. Their absence has negative predictive value, but their presence does not confirm the diagnosis. A positive HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 result does not confirm celiac disease, so it cannot replace serology and biopsy; its value is in a negative result. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac concordance in monozygotic twins is about 75% (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: celiac disease is associated with type 1 diabetes, thyroiditis, Sjögren syndrome, and IgA nephropathy (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A positive HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 result does not confirm celiac dis"}},{"id":"sdl29_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has bulky greasy stools and poor weight gain. Which phase of nutrient absorption is primarily impaired?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border digestion","B":"Intraluminal digestion","C":"Transepithelial transport","D":"Lymphatic lipid transport","E":"Mucosal nutrient uptake"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, which impairs the intraluminal phase of absorption. Oral enzyme supplements treat it. Brush-border (terminal) digestion is the phase lost in lactase deficiency, not pancreatic insufficiency. Cystic fibrosis causes pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, which impairs the intraluminal digestion phase, not transepithelial transport. Lymphatic transport is the last absorptive phase, and it is impaired by lymphatic obstruction rather than by the pancreatic insufficiency of cystic fibrosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Cystic fibrosis); Robbins Ch. 17 (Malabsorption and diarrhea, Table 17.6). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic insufficiency). COMLEX: malabsorption can disturb intraluminal digestion, terminal digestion, transepithelial transport, or lymphatic transport (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Brush-border (terminal) digestion is the phase lost in lacta","C":"Cystic fibrosis causes pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, wh","D":"Lymphatic transport is the last absorptive phase, and it is "}},{"id":"sdl29_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-month-old girl has steatorrhea and failure to thrive. Her blood smear shows acanthocytes, and plasma lacks apoB-containing lipoproteins. What will a small-bowel biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes","B":"Flattened villi with lymphocytes","C":"Neutrophils within the crypts","D":"PAS-positive foamy macrophages","E":"Absent ganglion cell clusters"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In abetalipoproteinemia, lipids are not assembled into lipoproteins and accumulate in enterocytes. The triglyceride shows as epithelial vacuolization, highlighted by oil red O after a fatty meal. Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytes describe celiac disease, not failed lipoprotein assembly. Neutrophilic mucosal infiltration characterizes IPEX autoimmune enteropathy rather than abetalipoproteinemia. PAS-positive foamy macrophages are seen in Whipple disease, whereas abetalipoproteinemia shows enterocytes vacuolated by lipid. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: failure to absorb essential fatty acids in abetalipoproteinemia causes fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia is autosomal recessive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytes describe ce","C":"Neutrophilic mucosal infiltration characterizes IPEX autoimm","D":"PAS-positive foamy macrophages are seen in Whipple disease, "}},{"id":"sdl29_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has severe persistent diarrhea, type 1 diabetes, and eczema. His maternal uncle died in infancy with similar findings. Which cell type is defective?","choices":{"A":"Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells","B":"Mature B lymphocytes","C":"CD4+ regulatory T cells","D":"Intestinal goblet cells","E":"Small-bowel enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IPEX is an X-linked autoimmune enteropathy in which loss of FOXP3, a transcription factor of CD4+ regulatory T cells, impairs their development and function. Neutrophils often infiltrate the mucosa, unlike celiac disease. Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells are the cytotoxic effectors that destroy enterocytes in celiac disease, not the FOXP3-dependent cells. Goblet cells are targets of the autoantibodies in IPEX, not the cells whose development fails. Enterocytes are attacked by autoantibodies in IPEX but are not the cell type that FOXP3 loss disables. IPEX is caused by mutations in FOXP3, a transcription factor needed by CD4+ regulatory T cells; B cells are not the defective population. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoantibodies to enterocytes and goblet cells are common in autoimmune enteropathy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: intraepithelial lymphocytes in autoimmune enteropathy increase less than in celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells are the cytotoxic effectors tha","D":"Goblet cells are targets of the autoantibodies in IPEX, not ","E":"Enterocytes are attacked by autoantibodies in IPEX but are n","B":"IPEX is caused by mutations in FOXP3, a transcription factor"}},{"id":"sdl29_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl from a rural community with poor sanitation in Zambia has stunted growth and malabsorption. No single pathogen is found, and antibiotics do not help. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Classic gluten-sensitive enteropathy","B":"Microvillus inclusion disease","C":"Congenital lipoprotein deficiency","D":"Inherited autoimmune enteropathy","E":"Environmental enteric dysfunction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Environmental enteric dysfunction occurs where sanitation is poor. It likely reflects chronic fecal pathogen exposure and repeated early diarrhea, no single agent is implicated, and antibiotics and supplements do not reverse it. Gluten-sensitive enteropathy is HLA-DQ2/DQ8-linked and shows more advanced villous atrophy than environmental enteric dysfunction. Congenital lipoprotein deficiency (abetalipoproteinemia) is an autosomal recessive MTP defect with acanthocytes, not a sanitation-linked enteropathy. Inherited autoimmune enteropathy (IPEX) is an X-linked FOXP3 defect with polyendocrinopathy, not an environmental disorder. Microvillus inclusion disease is an inherited MYO5B disorder that starts before 3 months of age, unlike a sanitation-linked enteropathy in a 3-year-old. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biopsies in environmental enteric dysfunction show more immune cell infiltration and less villous atrophy than advanced celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: stunting from environmental enteric dysfunction may be accompanied by uncorrectable cognitive deficits (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gluten-sensitive enteropathy is HLA-DQ2/DQ8-linked and shows","C":"Congenital lipoprotein deficiency (abetalipoproteinemia) is ","D":"Inherited autoimmune enteropathy (IPEX) is an X-linked FOXP3","B":"Microvillus inclusion disease is an inherited MYO5B disorder"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a painful, irreducible inguinal bulge. At the neck of the sac, what is compromised first?","choices":{"A":"Arterial inflow","B":"Lymph drainage","C":"Vagal supply","D":"Peritoneal fluid","E":"Venous drainage"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pressure at the neck of a hernia sac first impairs venous drainage. Stasis and edema then cause incarceration, then arterial and venous compromise (strangulation) and infarction. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hernias, Fig. 17.26). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inguinal hernias tend to have narrow orifices and large sacs (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: complicated hernias can present with tenderness, erythema, and fever (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hernias).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has her SMA blood flow restored after mesenteric thrombosis. When does most bowel damage occur?","choices":{"A":"Before the clot","B":"During reperfusion","C":"At first hypoxia","D":"After the scarring","E":"During fasting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ischemic injury occurs in two phases: initial hypoxic injury, then reperfusion, when blood supply returns and the greatest damage occurs. Bacterial products, free radicals, and neutrophils contribute. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emboli causing mesenteric occlusion most often arise from aortic atheromas or cardiac mural thrombi (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: necrotizing enterocolitis can cause pneumatosis intestinalis and portal venous gas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, necrotizing enterocolitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old man has bleeding from colonic vascular ectasias. What process likely produced them?","choices":{"A":"Repeated vein occlusion","B":"Portal hypertension","C":"Inherited telangiectasia","D":"Radiation damage","E":"Embolic infarction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normal distention and contraction may intermittently occlude submucosal veins crossing the muscularis propria. This leads to focal dilation and tortuosity of overlying vessels. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is associated with von Willebrand disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplastic vessels are vulnerable to disruption by even minor injury (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with celiac disease has new pain and weight loss despite a strict gluten-free diet. What should be suspected?","choices":{"A":"Lactose overload","B":"Dietary relapse","C":"Bile salt excess","D":"Refractory sprue","E":"Viral enteritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pain, diarrhea, and weight loss despite a strict gluten-free diet raise concern for refractory sprue, a possible precursor of T-cell lymphoma, or for cancer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a gluten-free diet usually restores near-normal histology within 6 to 24 months (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: celiac disease primarily affects the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old Chinese man has bloating and flatulence after milk. What will a duodenal biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Flattened villi","B":"Lipid vacuoles","C":"Unremarkable mucosa","D":"Crypt abscesses","E":"Foamy macrophages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acquired lactose intolerance comes from age-related downregulation of gene expression. Because the defect is biochemical, histology is generally unremarkable. Flattened villi indicate the villous atrophy of celiac disease. Lipid vacuoles in enterocytes are the triglyceride accumulation of abetalipoproteinemia. Neutrophilic crypt abscesses are a feature of ulcerative colitis. Distended foamy macrophages in the lamina propria indicate Whipple disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Lactase deficiency). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lactose hydrogen breath test is positive if breath hydrogen rises more than 20 ppm over baseline (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: congenital lactose intolerance is a rare autosomal recessive disorder (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion by amylase and brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Flattened villi indicate the villous atrophy of celiac disea","B":"Lipid vacuoles in enterocytes are the triglyceride accumulat","D":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses are a feature of ulcerative col","E":"Distended foamy macrophages in the lamina propria indicate W"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has intermittent obstruction from ileal intussusception. What most likely started it?","choices":{"A":"A mass lead point","B":"Bowel wall edema","C":"Recent rotavirus","D":"Midgut rotation","E":"Thin stool water"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In older children and adults, intussusception is usually caused by an intraluminal mass or tumor acting as the traction point, and surgery is needed. Lymphoid hyperplasia from viral infection or rotavirus vaccination underlies intussusception in young children, whereas adult cases arise from a mass lead point. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intussusception). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intussusception causes intermittent pain, vomiting, and bloody currant-jelly stools (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception). COMLEX: up to 1% of patients with cystic fibrosis develop intussusception (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — segmentation versus peristalsis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Lymphoid hyperplasia from viral infection or rotavirus vacci"}},{"id":"grid_sdl29_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":4,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy who lives in a rural village with poor sanitation has chronic diarrhea, bulky greasy stools, and stunted growth, and he failed to respond to two doses of an oral vaccine. He has no family history of gastrointestinal disease and eats a mixed diet that includes wheat. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Fecal fat","Villus height","Anti-TG2 IgA","Serum apoB"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↓","↑","↔"],"B":["↑","↔","↔","↔"],"C":["↑","↔","↔","↓"],"D":["↑","↓","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"D","explanation":"Choice D (↑ fecal fat, ↓ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is correct. Environmental enteric dysfunction (tropical sprue) follows chronic exposure to fecal pathogens where sanitation is poor; the notes list stunting and high oral vaccine failure rates, and describe pronounced lamina propria immune cell infiltration with villous atrophy that is less advanced than in celiac disease. Loss of absorptive surface causes malabsorption with steatorrhea, and the disorder is non-genetic and not driven by gluten, so celiac serology is negative. Choice A (↑ fecal fat, ↓ villus height, ↑ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is celiac disease, in which IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is the most sensitive serum marker. Choice B (↑ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, as in cystic fibrosis, where intraluminal digestion fails but the mucosa is intact. Choice C (↑ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↓ serum apoB) is abetalipoproteinemia, in which absent apoB-containing lipoproteins leave enterocytes unable to export chylomicrons, causing steatorrhea in infancy with acanthocytes on the smear. Choice E (↔ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is lactase deficiency, an osmotic diarrhea from unabsorbed lactose without fat malabsorption. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 29, sections 4 and 5: general malabsorption, cystic fibrosis, celiac disease, EED, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia); Robbins Ch. 17 (malabsorption and diarrhea). The ↑ fecal fat for environmental enteric dysfunction is inferred from loss of absorptive surface, and intact villi in the pancreatic, abetalipoproteinemia, and lactase disorders are inferred because the sources describe no villous injury in them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous atrophy with negative celiac serology in a child from an area of poor sanitation points to environmental enteric dysfunction (Exam 2 notes, SDL 29). COMLEX: steatorrhea with normal villi suggests a luminal (pancreatic) or export (abetalipoproteinemia) defect rather than mucosal loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — micelle formation and chylomicron export","isHighYield":true}]};
